window.PREVIEW_MOCKS["jammu-psy"] = {"id": "jammu-psy", "title": "University of Jammu, year not printed", "fullTitle": "University of Jammu Psychology entrance, year and booklet code not printed (real paper)", "sub": "60 real questions · year not printed · no official key, every answer worked out from standard texts · untimed", "sectionLabels": {"general": "General and Cognitive Psychology", "assessment": "Psychological Assessment", "social": "Social Psychology", "personality": "Personality", "physio": "Biopsychology", "dev": "Developmental Psychology", "clinical": "Clinical Psychology, Guidance and Counselling", "disorders": "Psychopathology", "research": "Research Methods and Statistics"}, "questions": [{"q": "Guidance means:", "options": {"a": "Advice", "b": "Assistance", "c": "Direction", "d": "None of the three"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: guidance means assistance, help offered to a person so that he can understand himself, make his own choices and solve his own problems. Lester D. Crow and Alice Crow (1951), in An Introduction to Guidance, stressed that guidance is not giving directions and not imposing one person's point of view on another. It is help given by trained people to a person of any age so that he can manage his own life, form his own views, make his own decisions and carry his own burdens. The aim is self-direction: a well-guided person needs less guidance over time. Guidance works by giving the person the facts he needs (about his abilities, his interests and the options open to him) and helping him weigh them. Example: Arjun, in Class 10, cannot decide between science and commerce. A counsellor gives him an aptitude test, shares information on courses and careers and talks through his interests; Arjun then makes the choice himself. If the counsellor simply said 'take science', that would be direction, not guidance. Option b is right because guidance is assistance that leaves the decision with the person being helped. Exam trap: in everyday speech 'guidance' sounds like advice or instruction, so a or c looks tempting; but guidance textbooks define it against both, since advice and direction keep the decision with the helper.", "topic": "Meaning of guidance", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a probability sample?", "options": {"a": "Quota", "b": "Purposive", "c": "Both (A) and (B)", "d": "Random"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: both quota and purposive sampling are non-probability samples. In probability sampling every member of the population has a known, non-zero chance of being chosen, and chance alone decides who is picked, so sampling error can be estimated. Its forms are simple random, systematic, stratified random, cluster and multistage sampling. In non-probability sampling the researcher's judgement or convenience decides who gets in, so some members have no chance at all and the results cannot be safely generalised. Quota sampling fixes how many people to take from each category (say 50 men and 50 women), much like stratified sampling, but the interviewer then picks whoever is handy within each quota. Purposive (judgemental) sampling means the researcher hand-picks cases he thinks typical or informative. Example: Meera wants to study teachers' stress. If she numbers all 500 teachers in her district and draws 100 by lottery, that is a random (probability) sample. If she interviews the first 20 male and 20 female teachers she meets, that is quota; if she chooses ten teachers she knows to be very experienced, that is purposive. Option c is right because neither quota nor purposive selection gives each member a known chance of being chosen. Exam trap: quota sampling looks like stratified random sampling, since both divide the population into categories; the difference is that stratified sampling draws randomly within each stratum, while quota sampling lets the interviewer choose.", "topic": "Probability and non-probability sampling", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Abnormality is characterised by ______ deviation.", "options": {"a": "Negative", "b": "Positive", "c": "Positive and negative", "d": "None of the three"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: abnormality, in the statistical sense, is deviation from the average in either direction, positive or negative. The statistical criterion defines normal as what is common and abnormal as what is rare. Many human traits, such as intelligence, height or anxiety, follow the normal (bell-shaped) curve: most people cluster near the mean, and fewer appear as you move out towards either tail. Anyone far from the mean, at either end, is statistically abnormal. Example: on IQ tests with a mean of 100 and an SD of 15, about 2% of people score below 70 and about 2% score above 130. Kabir, with an IQ of 65, and Riya, with an IQ of 145, are both statistically abnormal, though only Kabir's deviation is a problem. Option c is right because the deviation view of abnormality measures distance from the norm on both sides. The weakness of this criterion shows in Riya's case: it labels desirable rarities (genius, unusual calm, great musical talent) abnormal. So abnormal psychology adds other criteria, often summed up as the four Ds: deviance from social norms, distress, dysfunction (maladaptive behaviour) and danger. Exam trap: because 'abnormal' sounds bad, students pick negative deviation; but the statistical idea counts rarity, not harm, so both tails are abnormal. Key note: clinically only harmful deviations count as a disorder, so some would argue for a; but the deviation view of abnormality that the stem uses runs both ways from the norm, so c stands.", "topic": "Statistical criterion of abnormality", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In an experiment the variable which is manipulated is known as:", "options": {"a": "Dependent", "b": "Independent", "c": "Intervening", "d": "None of the three"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: the variable the experimenter manipulates is the independent variable (IV). An experiment asks whether changing one thing causes a change in another. The IV is the factor the researcher deliberately varies, such as the dose of a drug, the teaching method or the amount of noise. The dependent variable (DV) is the behaviour measured to see whether the IV had an effect; its value 'depends' on the IV. All other factors that could affect the DV, the extraneous variables (time of day, room temperature, the ability of the participants), are held constant or otherwise controlled. Example: Riya tests whether noise affects concentration. One group solves maths sums in a quiet room and another in a room with loud traffic noise. Noise level is the IV, since she manipulates it; the number of sums solved correctly is the DV, since she measures it. A third kind is the intervening variable, an unseen process inside the organism that links the IV and the DV, such as fatigue, hunger or expectancy. Edward C. Tolman (1938) used the term for such inferred processes, and Kenneth MacCorquodale and Paul Meehl (1948) later separated intervening variables from hypothetical constructs. Option b is right because manipulation by the experimenter is the defining feature of the IV. Exam trap: the IV and DV are easily reversed; remember 'I change the IV, I measure the DV', and the DV depends on the IV.", "topic": "Independent and dependent variables", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Psychology is the science of:", "options": {"a": "Soul", "b": "Consciousness", "c": "Mind", "d": "Behaviour"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: in its modern sense psychology is the science of behaviour, now usually stated as the scientific study of behaviour and mental processes. The word comes from the Greek psyche (soul) and logos (study), and its definition changed as the subject grew. Greek philosophers such as Aristotle (De Anima, about 350 BC) treated it as the study of the soul; but the soul cannot be observed or measured, so that definition was dropped. Later philosophers called it the science of the mind, which proved too vague. Wilhelm Wundt, who founded the first laboratory at Leipzig in 1879, made it the study of conscious experience, and William James (1890) called it 'the science of mental life'; but consciousness could only be studied by introspection, which is private and hard to check. John B. Watson (1913), in 'Psychology as the behaviorist views it', said psychology should study only observable behaviour, which others can measure and verify. Example: Meera's exam anxiety can be studied through what can be measured (her heart rate, her avoidance of revision, her anxiety test score), not through her 'soul'. Option d is right because behaviour is the observable, measurable subject matter that makes psychology a science, and it is the current definition among the four. Exam trap: the sequence soul, mind, consciousness, behaviour is a common question; soul is the oldest and behaviour the latest, so do not choose consciousness just because Wundt founded the science. Key note: the modern definition also includes mental processes, but of the four options behaviour is the current one, so d stands.", "topic": "Definitions of psychology", "fig": "John B. Watson"}, {"q": "Social Psychology is the scientific study of the way individuals ______ in a social situation.", "options": {"a": "think", "b": "feel", "c": "act", "d": "think, feel and act"}, "correct": "d", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is d: social psychology studies how individuals think, feel and act in social situations. Gordon Allport (1954), in the Handbook of Social Psychology, gave the classic definition: social psychology is an attempt to understand and explain how the thought, feeling and behaviour of individuals are influenced by the actual, imagined or implied presence of other human beings. 'Actual' means others are really there (a crowd watching you), 'imagined' means we picture them (thinking what our parents would say), and 'implied' means social roles and norms act on us even when we are alone. All three sides of the person are covered. Thinking: social cognition, such as how we form impressions, explain others' behaviour (attribution) and hold stereotypes. Feeling: attitudes, prejudice, attraction, love and the emotions others arouse in us. Acting: conformity, obedience, helping, aggression and behaviour in groups. Example: Kabir sees a classmate cheat in an exam. What he thinks (is it my business?), what he feels (anger, fear of being called a tell-tale) and what he does (reports it or keeps quiet) are all shaped by the people around him, and social psychology studies all three. Option d is right because the field covers cognition, emotion and behaviour together, not one of them alone. Exam trap: some older definitions speak only of the behaviour of individuals in social situations, which tempts students to pick c; but Allport's definition and modern textbooks name thought and feeling as well.", "topic": "Definition of social psychology", "fig": "Gordon Allport"}, {"q": "A person faces a problem of Depression, what type of guidance he needs?", "options": {"a": "Vocational", "b": "Educational", "c": "Personal", "d": "All the three"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: a person facing depression needs personal guidance. Guidance is usually divided into three main types. Educational guidance helps with choosing courses and subjects, study habits, examinations and learning difficulties. Vocational guidance helps with choosing a career, preparing for it and entering it; Frank Parsons (1909) laid its foundation. Personal guidance (sometimes called personal-social guidance) helps with emotional, social, family and health problems and with adjustment to oneself and to others. Depression, a lasting low mood with loss of interest, poor sleep, low energy and feelings of worthlessness, is an emotional problem of the person, so it falls under personal guidance. The guidance worker listens, supports and helps the person understand his feelings and find ways of coping; if the depression is clinical (severe, long-lasting or with thoughts of suicide), he refers the person to a clinical psychologist or psychiatrist for treatment. Example: Arjun, a college student, has felt sad and hopeless for months, has stopped meeting friends and has lost interest in everything. His problem is not which subject or which job to choose but his emotional state, so he needs personal guidance and probably a referral. Option c is right because depression is an emotional and adjustment problem, the domain of personal guidance. Exam trap: 'All the three' tempts because depression often lowers marks and work output; but educational and vocational guidance deal with study and career choices, the side effects, not the emotional problem itself.", "topic": "Types of guidance", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Types of Scales are:", "options": {"a": "Ordinal", "b": "Nominal", "c": "Interval", "d": "All the three"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: ordinal, nominal and interval are all types of measurement scale. Stanley Smith Stevens (1946), in his paper 'On the theory of scales of measurement' in Science, named four levels of measurement, each adding a property to the one below. Nominal: numbers or names only label categories, with no order (gender, blood group, roll numbers, house colours); you can count frequencies and find the mode. Ordinal: categories are ranked in order, but the gaps between ranks are not equal (class rank, socioeconomic class); the median and percentiles suit it. Interval: equal units between points, but the zero is arbitrary (temperature in Celsius; IQ scores are usually treated this way); the mean and SD can be used, but ratios such as 'twice as much' cannot. Ratio: equal units and a true zero (height, weight, reaction time, income); all statistics, including ratios, are meaningful. A memory aid is NOIR: nominal, ordinal, interval, ratio. Example: in a school race, Meera's bib number 27 is nominal, her finishing position of 2nd is ordinal, the temperature that day (30 degrees Celsius) is interval, and her time of 52 seconds is ratio. Option d is right because each of the three listed is one of Stevens' four scales; the fourth, ratio, is simply not offered. Exam trap: students sometimes think only the 'higher' scales count as real measurement; but nominal and ordinal are scales too, and the property that separates the top two is the zero (arbitrary in interval, true in ratio).", "topic": "Scales of measurement", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A belief that one is being spied on, threatened or otherwise mistreated is a delusion of:", "options": {"a": "Grandeur", "b": "Control", "c": "Persecution", "d": "None of the three"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: the belief that one is being spied on, threatened or otherwise mistreated is a delusion of persecution. A delusion is a fixed, false belief, held firmly despite clear evidence against it and not explained by the person's culture or religion. It is a disorder of the content of thought, unlike a hallucination, which is a disorder of perception. In a persecutory delusion the person believes he is being followed, watched, plotted against, poisoned, cheated or harassed, often by neighbours, family, the police or a secret group. It is the most common type of delusion and is seen in schizophrenia, in delusional disorder (persecutory type) and in severe mood disorders with psychotic features. Example: Kabir is sure his neighbours have hidden cameras in his walls and are mixing poison into his food; no evidence or reassurance shakes him, and he stops eating at home. That is a persecutory delusion. Other common types are grandeur (special powers, wealth or identity), control (an outside force controls one's thoughts or actions), reference (ordinary events carry special messages about oneself), jealousy (a partner's unfaithfulness) and nihilistic (oneself or the world does not exist). Option c is right because being spied on, threatened and mistreated is the defining content of persecution. Exam trap: delusions of reference often travel with persecution (the news is about me, people on the bus are talking about me), but the specific belief that one is being harmed or plotted against is persecutory.", "topic": "Delusion of persecution", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The first Psychological Laboratory was established in:", "options": {"a": "Boston", "b": "California", "c": "Berlin", "d": "Leipzig"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: the first psychological laboratory was established at Leipzig, Germany. Wilhelm Wundt set it up at the University of Leipzig in 1879, and that date is taken as the birth of psychology as a separate experimental science, independent of philosophy and physiology. In the laboratory Wundt and his students measured reaction times, studied sensation, perception and attention, and trained observers in introspection, careful reporting of conscious experience under controlled conditions. His approach, carried to America by his student Edward Titchener (at Cornell from 1892), became structuralism. Many early psychologists trained there, including James McKeen Cattell, who took his doctorate under Wundt in 1886, and Leipzig became the model for laboratories around the world. Example: a student like Riya in Wundt's laboratory would press a key the moment she heard a click, and her reaction time would be measured in thousandths of a second and compared across conditions. Option d is right because Leipzig, with Wundt's 1879 laboratory, is the standard answer for the first formal psychology laboratory. Exam trap: William James had a small demonstration room at Harvard (in Cambridge, next to Boston) by about 1875, so Boston is tempting; but it was used for teaching demonstrations, not systematic research, so Wundt's Leipzig laboratory is counted first. G. Stanley Hall opened the first American research laboratory at Johns Hopkins University, Baltimore, in 1883.", "topic": "First psychology laboratory", "fig": "Wilhelm Wundt"}, {"q": "Case study is related to ______ Psychology.", "options": {"a": "Clinical", "b": "Experimental", "c": "General", "d": "None of the three"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: the case study is the method most closely tied to clinical psychology. A case study is an intensive, in-depth study of one individual (sometimes one family or group), built up from many sources: a detailed case history (family background, childhood, education, health, relationships), interviews with the person and those close to him, observation, psychological tests, and records such as school reports or medical files. Clinical psychology assesses and treats one person's problems at a time, so the method suits it exactly: the clinician must understand this person's history to diagnose the problem and plan treatment. Lightner Witmer opened the first psychological clinic at the University of Pennsylvania in 1896 and coined the term 'clinical psychology'. Sigmund Freud built psychoanalysis on case studies such as Anna O. (with Josef Breuer, 1895) and Little Hans (1909). Example: Meera, aged 9, refuses to go to school. The psychologist takes a case history from her parents, talks to her teacher, observes her and gives tests, then writes up the whole picture with a treatment plan. Option a is right because the case study is the core method of clinical work, which studies one person in depth for diagnosis and treatment. Exam trap: case studies also appear in neuropsychology (Phineas Gage, the patient H.M.) and in research, but their weakness, that one case cannot be generalised, keeps them from being the main method of experimental psychology, which relies on controlled experiments with groups.", "topic": "Case study method", "fig": ""}, {"q": "To understand the child rearing practices of tribal people, the best method is:", "options": {"a": "Participatory observation", "b": "Field Survey", "c": "Interview", "d": "All the three"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: participant observation is the best method for studying the child-rearing practices of tribal people. In participant observation the researcher lives in the community for a long time, takes part in its daily life, and watches behaviour from the inside as it happens naturally. Over months the people get used to him and act normally. It is the classic method of anthropology and ethnography. Bronislaw Malinowski (1922) lived among the Trobriand Islanders to write Argonauts of the Western Pacific, and Margaret Mead (1928) lived in Samoa for Coming of Age in Samoa, a study of adolescence and upbringing. Child rearing suits this method well: feeding, weaning, toilet training, discipline and play happen every day at home, are partly unspoken habits, and parents may not describe them accurately. Tribal people may also be unused to forms and wary of outsiders asking questions. Example: Kabir, a research student, spends six months in a Bhil village, helps with daily chores and records how mothers carry, feed and discipline their children. Option a is right because only participant observation records what tribal parents actually do, in their natural setting, from the inside. Exam trap: surveys and interviews record what people say they do, not what they actually do, and they need a shared language and trust. Key note: anthropologists combine observation with interviews, so d is tempting; but the stem asks for the best single method, and participant observation is the textbook answer for studying a culture from inside, so a stands.", "topic": "Participant observation", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Establishment of Central Bureau of Educational and Vocational Guidance in 1954 was the result of the recommendations of:", "options": {"a": "Secondary Education Commission", "b": "Kothari Commission", "c": "University Education Commission", "d": "None of the three"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: the Central Bureau of Educational and Vocational Guidance (1954) was set up as a result of the recommendations of the Secondary Education Commission. After independence, India appointed commissions to review its education system. The Secondary Education Commission (1952 to 1953), chaired by Dr A. Lakshmanaswami Mudaliar and so also called the Mudaliar Commission, studied secondary schooling. It recommended that educational and vocational guidance become part of secondary education, with trained guidance officers and career masters made available to schools. Acting on this, the Ministry of Education set up the Central Bureau of Educational and Vocational Guidance in Delhi in 1954 to train guidance workers and prepare tests and career information; state guidance bureaus followed, and the Central Bureau was later absorbed into the National Council of Educational Research and Training (NCERT). Example: when a school counsellor gives Riya, in Class 9, an aptitude test and talks her through the choice of stream, she is doing the work the Mudaliar Commission wanted in every secondary school. Option a is right because the Mudaliar Commission reported in 1953, just before the Bureau was founded, and its report made guidance a formal recommendation for secondary schools. Exam trap: the Kothari Commission is the best-known Indian education commission and also stressed guidance, so students pick b; but it sat from 1964 to 1966, a decade after the Bureau was founded, so it cannot be the cause.", "topic": "Guidance movement in India", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A student's rank in a class involves the scale:", "options": {"a": "Nominal", "b": "Ordinal", "c": "Interval", "d": "Ratio"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: a student's rank in class is measured on an ordinal scale. An ordinal scale puts people or objects in order of more or less of something, but it does not tell how far apart they are. Rank 1 is above rank 2 and rank 2 above rank 3, yet the gaps need not be equal. Example: in a class test, Riya scores 95 and comes first, Arjun scores 94 and comes second, and Meera scores 74 and comes third. The ranks 1, 2, 3 look evenly spaced, but the real gap between first and second is 1 mark and between second and third is 20 marks. So ranks show position, not amount. This scheme comes from Stanley Smith Stevens (1946), who named four scales: nominal (labels only), ordinal (order), interval (equal units, arbitrary zero) and ratio (equal units, true zero). Statistics suited to ordinal data are the median, percentiles and rank-order correlation such as Charles Spearman's (1904) rho, rather than the mean. Option b is right because ranking gives order without equal intervals, which is the definition of ordinal measurement. Exam trap: ranks are written as numbers, so students think they are interval; but numbers do not make a scale interval unless the units between them are equal. The marks themselves are a higher level of measurement; the ranks made from them are ordinal.", "topic": "Ordinal scale", "fig": ""}, {"q": "During hallucinations a person:", "options": {"a": "Sees the things that are not really there", "b": "Hears things that are not really there", "c": "Both (A) and (B)", "d": "None of the three"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: during hallucinations a person may both see and hear things that are not really there. A hallucination is a perception that occurs without any external stimulus, yet feels as real and vivid as a normal perception and seems to come from outside the person. It can occur in any sense. Auditory hallucinations (hearing voices that comment on the person or give commands) are the most common type in schizophrenia. Visual hallucinations (seeing people, animals or lights) are common in delirium, drug states and some dementias. There are also tactile ones (feeling insects crawling under the skin, called formication, as in cocaine use or alcohol withdrawal), olfactory ones (smells) and gustatory ones (tastes). Example: Arjun, in severe alcohol withdrawal, sees small animals running across the wall and hears his late father calling his name, though nothing is there; both experiences are hallucinations. Option c is right because hallucinations are not limited to one sense, so seeing and hearing things that are not present are both hallucinations. Exam trap: a hallucination is easily confused with an illusion and with a delusion. An illusion is a misperception of something real (a rope at dusk seen as a snake), and a delusion is a false belief, not a perception. If there is no stimulus at all, it is a hallucination.", "topic": "Hallucinations", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Controls are must in design:", "options": {"a": "One group", "b": "Parallel group", "c": "Rotation group", "d": "All the three"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: controls are a must in all three designs, the one group, the parallel group and the rotation group design. Control is the essence of an experiment. To show that the independent variable (IV) caused a change in the dependent variable (DV), the researcher must compare the experimental condition with a control condition and hold all other (extraneous) variables constant. There are three classic designs. In the one group (single group) design, the same group is tested first under the control condition and then under the experimental condition, so it serves as its own control. In the parallel group (equivalent group) design, two groups are made equal by matching or random assignment: the experimental group receives the IV and the control group does not. In the rotation group design, two groups swap conditions in turn, so each serves once as the experimental and once as the control group, which cancels out differences between the groups. Example: Meera tests whether music helps memory. In the rotation design, group A learns word lists with music first and group B in silence first, then they switch. Option d is right because each design builds in a control condition and needs extraneous variables controlled. Exam trap: the one group design looks as if it has no control because there is no second group; in fact its own control-condition scores are the comparison. Key note: if 'controls' is read as a separate control group, only the parallel group design has one, so b is tempting; but each of the three designs builds in its own control condition and control of extraneous variables is a must in all of them, so d stands.", "topic": "Experimental designs and control", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Sociability as a trait is mostly determined by:", "options": {"a": "Heredity", "b": "Environment", "c": "Both (A) and (B)", "d": "None of the three"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: sociability as a trait is determined by both heredity and environment. Sociability is the tendency to seek out and enjoy the company of others, a central part of extraversion. Arnold Buss and Robert Plomin (1984) listed it, with emotionality and activity, as one of three temperaments (the EAS model) that appear early in life and are partly inherited. Twin studies show the genetic part: identical twins, who share all their genes, are more alike in sociability and extraversion than fraternal twins, who share about half. The Minnesota Study of Twins Reared Apart, reported by Thomas Bouchard and colleagues (1990), found identical twins raised in different homes about as alike in personality as those raised together, with heritability of about 0.40 to 0.50. So roughly half the variation between people is genetic, and the rest comes from the environment: family, friends, school, culture and chance experiences. Jerome Kagan (1988) found that very shy, inhibited toddlers often stayed cautious, yet many became less shy as they grew. Heredity sets a range; environment decides where in that range a child ends up. Example: Riya was a shy baby like her mother, but her parents took her to play groups and a drama club, and she grew into a fairly sociable teenager. Option c is right because sociability is about half heritable and half shaped by experience. Exam trap: the word 'mostly' pushes students to pick one side; but a heritability near 0.50 means neither heredity nor environment dominates, so both is the standard answer.", "topic": "Heredity, environment and sociability", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Socialisation can be best facilitated by:", "options": {"a": "Indoor games", "b": "Outdoor games", "c": "Excursions", "d": "None of the three"}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: socialisation is best facilitated by outdoor games. Socialisation is the process by which a child learns the values, norms, roles and social skills of his group and becomes a functioning member of society. The family starts it; peers, school and play carry it further. Mildred Parten (1932) showed that play itself becomes more social with age, moving from solitary and parallel play (alone, or beside others) to associative play (playing together and sharing materials) and finally cooperative play, in which children share a goal and take roles. Outdoor team games such as kabaddi, kho-kho, cricket and football are cooperative play at its fullest. They bring many children together every day, and each game demands social skills: cooperating with teammates, taking turns, following rules, accepting the umpire's decision, leading and following, controlling anger and accepting defeat. Example: Kabir, a shy boy, joins the school kho-kho team. Within months he has learned to plan moves with teammates, wait his turn and shake hands after losing, skills he never picked up from video games at home. Option b is right because outdoor team games give the most regular, rule-bound group interaction, which is what socialisation needs. Exam trap: excursions sound social because the whole class goes together, but they are occasional, so their effect is small compared with daily play. Key note: excursions and indoor games also help socialisation; outdoor team games are the textbook answer because they give the most regular group interaction, so b stands.", "topic": "Play and socialisation", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Frank Parsons is associated with ______ guidance.", "options": {"a": "Educational", "b": "Vocational", "c": "Personal", "d": "All the three"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: Frank Parsons is associated with vocational guidance; he is called the father of vocational guidance. Frank Parsons (1854 to 1908), a Boston engineer, lawyer and social reformer, opened the Vocation Bureau in Boston in 1908 to help young people, many of them poor or immigrants, choose suitable work instead of drifting into whatever job came first. His book Choosing a Vocation appeared in 1909, after his death. In it he set out the first model of career choice, later called the trait-and-factor approach, in three steps: (1) a clear understanding of yourself, your aptitudes, abilities, interests and limitations; (2) knowledge of the requirements, conditions, pay and prospects of different kinds of work; and (3) 'true reasoning' on the relation between these two sets of facts, that is, matching the person to the job. This matching idea still underlies career counselling and John Holland's (1959) theory of matching personality types to work environments, later known as the RIASEC types. Example: Meera, good at drawing and interested in buildings, learns what architecture demands and offers, and decides it fits her; that is Parsons' three-step method in action. Option b is right because Parsons' work was about choosing an occupation, which is vocational guidance. Exam trap: educational guidance is a different strand credited to others: Jesse B. Davis brought guidance into the Grand Rapids schools in 1907, and Truman Kelley (1914) first used the term 'educational guidance'.", "topic": "Frank Parsons, vocational guidance", "fig": "Frank Parsons"}, {"q": "Ratio scales have:", "options": {"a": "True zero", "b": "Arbitrary zero", "c": "Both (A) and (B)", "d": "None of the three"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: ratio scales have a true (absolute) zero. On a ratio scale zero means a complete absence of the quantity being measured, and the units are equal all along the scale. Because zero is real, ratios make sense: 10 kg is twice 5 kg, and a reaction time of 400 milliseconds is twice one of 200 milliseconds. Examples are height, weight, age, income, reaction time and the number of errors made. The interval scale also has equal units, but its zero is arbitrary, a point fixed by convention: 0 degrees Celsius is the freezing point of water, not 'no temperature', so 40 degrees is not twice as hot as 20 degrees. IQ is treated the same way: an IQ of 0 would not mean no intelligence, and an IQ of 140 is not twice an IQ of 70. These levels were set out by Stanley Smith Stevens (1946), who ordered the four scales nominal, ordinal, interval and ratio, with ratio the highest because it has every property. Example: Arjun takes 30 seconds to solve a puzzle and Riya takes 60; saying Riya took twice as long is meaningful because time has a true zero. Option a is right because a true zero is the property that sets the ratio scale apart from the interval scale. Exam trap: students mix up the two scales that have equal units; remember that interval has an arbitrary zero and ratio a true one, and no scale can have both kinds of zero.", "topic": "Ratio scale and true zero", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A patient washes hands repeatedly in:", "options": {"a": "Anxiety", "b": "Hysteria", "c": "Obsessive-compulsive neurosis", "d": "All the three"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: repeated hand washing is the classic compulsion of obsessive-compulsive neurosis, now called obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD). OCD has two parts. Obsessions are intrusive, unwanted thoughts, images or urges that cause anxiety, such as the idea that one's hands are dirty or contaminated. Compulsions are repetitive acts or mental rituals done to reduce that anxiety, such as washing, checking, counting or ordering. The relief is brief, so the ritual returns and grows. Contamination fear with washing is the commonest picture. Example: Kabir washes his hands 40 times a day after touching door handles; he knows the fear is excessive, yet feels unbearable tension if he stops. The old name, obsessive-compulsive neurosis, is the term used in the second Diagnostic and Statistical Manual (DSM-II, 1968), which listed it among the neuroses. DSM-III (1980) to DSM-IV-TR placed OCD among the anxiety disorders, and DSM-5 (2013) gave it its own chapter, Obsessive-Compulsive and Related Disorders, with body dysmorphic disorder, hoarding, trichotillomania and excoriation. Treatment is exposure and response prevention (touching the 'dirty' object without washing) and SSRI medicines. Option c is right because the stem describes a compulsion, the defining sign of OCD. Exam trap: hysteria is the old name for conversion and dissociative disorders, not for rituals, and 'anxiety' is the broad family, not the specific diagnosis. Key note: OCD was classed as an anxiety disorder from DSM-III to DSM-IV-TR, so a is tempting; but repeated hand washing is the hallmark compulsion of OCD itself, so the answer used here, c, stands.", "topic": "Obsessive-compulsive disorder", "fig": ""}, {"q": "If one parent provides dominant gene for brown hair colour and the other gives recessive gene for the brown hair, the offspring will have:", "options": {"a": "Brown hair", "b": "Black hair", "c": "Both (A) and (B)", "d": "None of the three"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: the child will have brown hair, because a dominant allele shows itself even when it is paired with a recessive one. Gregor Mendel (1866), crossing pea plants, found that each trait is set by a pair of inherited factors, now called genes, one from each parent. The alternative forms of a gene are alleles. Two different alleles make a person heterozygous. In dominant-recessive inheritance the heterozygote shows only the dominant allele's trait (the phenotype); the recessive allele is masked but carried silently, so the child is a carrier who may pass it to a later generation. Example: Meera's father gives her the dominant brown allele (B) and her mother a recessive allele (b). Meera is Bb, so her hair is brown; if she later has children with another carrier, one child in four (bb) could show the recessive trait. Option a is right because a Bb child expresses the dominant brown allele. Exam trap: 'both' would need codominance (blood group AB shows both A and B) or incomplete dominance (red and white snapdragons giving pink); when one allele is stated to be dominant there is no mixing, and only the dominant trait shows. Key note: the stem is loosely worded ('recessive gene for the brown hair' means the recessive allele of the hair-colour gene), and real hair colour is polygenic, but on the one-gene textbook model the dominant brown allele shows, so the answer used here, a, stands.", "topic": "Mendel's law of dominance", "fig": "Gregor Mendel"}, {"q": "Identity crisis is related to:", "options": {"a": "Infancy", "b": "Childhood", "c": "Adolescence", "d": "Adulthood"}, "correct": "c", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is c: identity crisis belongs to adolescence, the fifth of Erik Erikson's eight psychosocial stages. Erik Erikson (1950, Childhood and Society; 1968, Identity: Youth and Crisis) described life as eight stages, each with a 'crisis', a turning point between two outcomes, set by the meeting of the growing person with society's demands. In adolescence (about 12 to 18 years) the crisis is identity versus role confusion: the young person asks 'Who am I? What do I believe? What will I become?' and tries out roles, friendships, values and career ideas. Success gives fidelity, a stable sense of self; failure leaves role confusion. Erikson himself coined the term 'identity crisis'. Example: Arjun, 16, swings between wanting to be a cricketer, an engineer and a musician, and argues with his parents about religion; this trying out is normal identity work, not illness. James Marcia (1966) later sorted adolescents into four identity statuses: diffusion, foreclosure, moratorium and achievement. Option c is right because Erikson placed the identity crisis in adolescence. Exam trap: every stage has its own crisis, and examiners swap them: infancy is trust versus mistrust, early childhood autonomy versus shame and doubt, the play age initiative versus guilt, the school age industry versus inferiority, young adulthood intimacy versus isolation, adulthood generativity versus stagnation, and old age integrity versus despair.", "topic": "Erikson's identity crisis", "fig": "Erik Erikson"}, {"q": "A student is least liked by the classmates in the class, he is:", "options": {"a": "Popular", "b": "Star", "c": "Isolate", "d": "None of the three"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: in sociometry, a student whom classmates do not choose is an isolate, the low-acceptance position in the group. Jacob L. Moreno (1934, Who Shall Survive?) devised sociometry to map attraction and rejection inside a group. Each member names, privately, the classmates he would most like to sit, play or work with (and sometimes those he would not). The choices are drawn as a sociogram, with circles for members and arrows for choices. A star receives many choices and is the most popular; an isolate receives none; a neglectee receives only a few; a rejectee is actively rejected by many; a mutual pair choose each other, and a clique chooses only within itself. Teachers use the sociogram to find lonely children and to plan seating and group work. Example: in Class 8, Riya is named by 15 classmates (a star), while Sameer is named by no one (an isolate); the class teacher pairs Sameer with a friendly classmate for projects. Option c is right because, of the options, isolate is the only sociometric label for a child at the bottom of peer acceptance. Exam trap: popular and star both describe the most liked child, the exact opposite of the stem. Key note: strictly, a child actively disliked is a 'rejectee' and an isolate is one nobody chooses; rejectee is not offered, and of the options isolate is the low-acceptance position, so the answer used here, c, stands.", "topic": "Sociometry: star and isolate", "fig": "Jacob L. Moreno"}, {"q": "In guidance, appraisal of client is needed:", "options": {"a": "Hardly", "b": "Not at all", "c": "Always", "d": "Sometimes"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: appraisal of the client is needed always in guidance, because no one can be guided without first being understood. Appraisal (also called the individual inventory or student appraisal service) is the collection and study of information about the person: intelligence, aptitude, achievement and interest tests, the cumulative record, interviews, observation, rating scales, autobiography and the case study. It is the base on which the other guidance services stand: orientation, information, counselling, placement and follow-up. Frank Parsons (1909, Choosing a Vocation) made it the first of his three steps: a clear understanding of yourself, your aptitudes, abilities and interests; then knowledge of the world of work; then true reasoning to match the two. Appraisal is also continuous, not a one-time event, because a pupil's abilities, interests and problems change as he grows. Example: before advising Neha on choosing science or commerce after Class 10, the counsellor studies her marks over three years, an aptitude test, an interest inventory and a talk with her parents; without these, the advice would be guesswork. Option c is right because every guidance decision rests on knowing the individual, so appraisal is needed in every case and all through the process. Exam trap: 'sometimes' sounds moderate and wise, but guidance texts treat appraisal as the foundation of all guidance, not an optional extra used only for difficult cases.", "topic": "Appraisal in guidance", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which is most important in test construction?", "options": {"a": "Reliability", "b": "Validity", "c": "Objectivity", "d": "All the three"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: validity is the most important quality in test construction. Validity is the degree to which a test measures what it claims to measure. The Standards for Educational and Psychological Testing, issued jointly by the American Educational Research Association, the American Psychological Association and the National Council on Measurement in Education (1999 and 2014 editions), call validity the most fundamental consideration in developing and evaluating tests. Reliability is consistency: the test gives about the same result on repeated use. Objectivity is agreement between scorers, free of the examiner's personal judgement. Both are needed, but they serve validity. Reliability is necessary but not sufficient: a test can be reliable without being valid, yet it cannot be valid without being reliable, because the validity coefficient cannot exceed the square root of the reliability coefficient. Example: Arjun's teacher builds a 'memory test' whose scores in fact depend mostly on reading speed; pupils get almost the same scores each time (reliable) and every scorer agrees (objective), yet it tells her little about memory (not valid). In the same way, head size can be measured very consistently, but it is worthless as a test of intelligence. Option b is right because a test that does not measure what it claims is worthless, however consistent or objective it is. Exam trap: 'All the three' looks safe because a good test needs reliability, objectivity, validity, norms and usability; but the stem asks for the most important one. Key note: a good test needs all three, so d is tempting, and some Indian texts list objectivity first; but the stem asks which is most important, and most textbooks and the testing Standards name validity, so the answer used here, b, stands.", "topic": "Validity in test construction", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Fear of darkness is known as:", "options": {"a": "Acrophobia", "b": "Agoraphobia", "c": "Zoophobia", "d": "Nyctophobia"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: fear of darkness (or of the night) is nyctophobia, from the Greek nyx, night, and phobos, fear. A phobia is an intense, irrational fear of a specific object or situation, out of proportion to the real danger, that leads to avoidance. In DSM-5-TR this is a specific phobia: marked fear or anxiety almost always set off by the object or situation, which is avoided or endured with intense distress, usually lasting 6 months or more and causing distress or impairment. Fear of the dark is common and normal in young children; it becomes a phobia when it persists and disrupts life. Example: Kabir, 22, cannot sleep without a light on and refuses to travel at night; even the thought of a power cut makes his heart race. Phobia names are built from Greek roots: acro (peak, heights), agora (marketplace), zoo (animal), claustro (closed space), hydro (water), nycto (night). Treatment is graded exposure, as in systematic desensitisation, developed by Joseph Wolpe (1958). Option d is right because nycto means night, so nyctophobia is fear of the dark. Exam trap: agoraphobia is often misread as fear of the dark or of strangers; it is fear of places where escape is hard, such as crowds, open markets and public transport, and DSM-5 lists it as a separate anxiety disorder, not a specific phobia.", "topic": "Naming specific phobias", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Intelligence is related to a gland:", "options": {"a": "Thyroid", "b": "Pituitary", "c": "Adrenals", "d": "Gonads"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: the thyroid is the gland most directly tied to intelligence, because its hormone, thyroxine, is essential for brain development. The thyroid, in the neck, releases thyroxine (T4) and triiodothyronine (T3), which set the body's metabolic rate and, in the foetus and infant, support the growth of neurons, myelin and synapses. Too little thyroid hormone from birth (congenital hypothyroidism, once called cretinism) causes intellectual disability and stunted growth unless it is treated in the first weeks of life, which is why newborns are screened for it. The thyroid needs iodine to make thyroxine, and the World Health Organization calls iodine deficiency the world's most common preventable cause of brain damage; India iodises salt for this reason. In adults, hypothyroidism slows thinking and causes lethargy (myxoedema), while hyperthyroidism (Graves' disease) causes anxiety and restlessness. Example: baby Aarav's newborn screening shows low thyroxine; daily thyroxine tablets started at once let him develop normally. Option a is right because a shortage of the thyroid's own hormone in early life directly impairs intellectual development. Exam trap: the pituitary is the 'master gland', so it is often picked; but its own growth hormone governs height (gigantism, dwarfism), not intelligence. Key note: the pituitary controls the thyroid through thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH), so b is tempting; but the gland whose own hormone deficit directly causes intellectual disability is the thyroid, so the answer used here, a, stands.", "topic": "Thyroid and intelligence", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Chimpanzee is to Kohler as cat is to:", "options": {"a": "Thorndike", "b": "Skinner", "c": "Pavlov", "d": "Hull"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: chimpanzees go with Wolfgang Kohler and cats with Edward Thorndike, the classic animals of insight learning and trial-and-error learning. Edward Thorndike (1898, Animal Intelligence) shut hungry cats in puzzle boxes; a cat could reach food outside only by pulling a loop or pressing a lever. At first it scratched and clawed at random, and by accident it hit the latch. Over trials the useless moves dropped out and escape time fell gradually: trial-and-error learning. He summed this up in the law of effect: responses followed by satisfaction are 'stamped in', those followed by discomfort 'stamped out'. Wolfgang Kohler (1917 in German; The Mentality of Apes, 1925 in English), working on Tenerife from 1913 to 1920, placed bananas out of reach of chimpanzees; Sultan joined two sticks to rake them in, and others stacked boxes, suddenly, after a pause. This was insight learning, a sudden grasp of the relations in a problem. Example: Ananya learns a new phone by pressing buttons until one works (Thorndike's trial and error), but solves a riddle in a flash of understanding (Kohler's insight). Option a is right because Thorndike's cats in puzzle boxes are as tied to him as chimpanzees are to Kohler. Exam trap: examiners shuffle the animal pairings; Ivan Pavlov's animal was the dog, B. F. Skinner's the rat and the pigeon.", "topic": "Thorndike's cats, Kohler's apes", "fig": "Edward Thorndike"}, {"q": "Barrier/s to group cohesiveness is/are:", "options": {"a": "Different languages", "b": "Different castes", "c": "Both (A) and (B)", "d": "Same religion"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: both different languages and different castes are barriers to group cohesiveness. Group cohesiveness is the strength of the bonds that hold members in a group: how much they like the group, each other and its goals. Leon Festinger, Stanley Schachter and Kurt Back (1950) defined it as 'the total field of forces which act on members to remain in the group'. Cohesion rises with similarity of attitudes and background, shared goals, frequent interaction, small size, an outside threat and success; it falls with anything that divides members. Different languages block communication: members cannot share ideas, jokes or plans easily, and they drift into language subgroups. Different castes bring status divisions and prejudice, so members sort themselves into in-groups and out-groups and trust each other less. Same religion, by contrast, is a shared similarity, which tends to bind members together. Example: in a hostel cricket team, Tamil-speaking and Hindi-speaking players who cannot talk freely, and players who keep to their own caste circles, pass and plan less well than a team that shares a language and mixes freely. Option c is right because each of a and b splits the group into subgroups and weakens the forces holding it together. Exam trap: 'same religion' is planted as a distractor; similarity raises cohesion, so it supports a group rather than blocking it.", "topic": "Group cohesiveness", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In order to deal with educational problems counsellor needs:", "options": {"a": "Case study", "b": "Cumulative Record Card", "c": "Interview", "d": "None of the three"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: to deal with a pupil's educational problems, the counsellor needs the cumulative record card. The cumulative record card is a continuous, year-by-year record of a pupil's development kept by the school: marks in each subject, attendance, intelligence, aptitude and achievement test scores, health, family background, co-curricular activities, personality traits and teachers' remarks. Because it builds up over the years and moves with the pupil from class to class, it shows trends that a single meeting cannot: a fall in marks after a change of school, a long illness, a gap between ability and achievement. That makes it the main tool of educational guidance: diagnosing backwardness and underachievement, choosing subjects and streams, and spotting learning difficulties. Example: Rohan's maths marks fell from 80 in Class 6 to 45 in Class 8; his card shows average intelligence and many absences through illness in Class 7, so the counsellor arranges remedial maths instead of labelling him 'weak'. Option b is right because the card holds the pupil's whole school history in one place, which is exactly what educational problems need. Exam trap: the case study is an in-depth study of one problem case that uses the cumulative record as one of its sources, and the interview is a face-to-face talk; both help, but neither is the standing school record. Key note: case study and interview also help a counsellor; but for educational problems the cumulative record card is the core tool, as it holds the pupil's whole school history in one place, so the answer used here, b, stands.", "topic": "Cumulative record card", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Reliability is related to:", "options": {"a": "Test-retest", "b": "Odd-even", "c": "Both (A) and (B)", "d": "None of the three"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: test-retest and odd-even (split-half) are both methods of estimating reliability. Reliability is the consistency of test scores: a reliable test gives about the same result when the same people are tested again, or when equivalent sets of items are compared. It is expressed as a reliability coefficient, a correlation that runs from 0 to 1. In the test-retest method the same test is given twice to the same group after an interval (say two weeks) and the two sets of scores are correlated; this gives the coefficient of stability. In the odd-even method the test is given once, split into odd-numbered and even-numbered items, and the two half-scores are correlated; because each half is only half as long as the full test, the result is stepped up with the Spearman-Brown prophecy formula (Charles Spearman and William Brown, 1910). This is a measure of internal consistency. Other methods are parallel (alternate) forms, the Kuder-Richardson formulas (G. Frederic Kuder and Marion Richardson, 1937), Lee Cronbach's (1951) coefficient alpha and agreement between raters. Example: Ms Lata gives her class of 40 a vocabulary test; correlating each pupil's March and April scores is test-retest, while correlating each pupil's odd-item and even-item totals from one sitting is split-half. Option c is right because each listed method yields a reliability coefficient. Exam trap: validity is a different question (does the test measure the right thing?), and its types are content, criterion-related and construct validity, not retesting or splitting items.", "topic": "Methods of estimating reliability", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Hypochondria is a type of:", "options": {"a": "Psychosis", "b": "Psychoneurosis", "c": "Both (A) and (B)", "d": "None of the three"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: hypochondria (hypochondriasis) is classed as a psychoneurosis, a neurotic disorder. Hypochondriasis is a persistent preoccupation with having, or getting, a serious illness despite normal medical tests and reassurance; ordinary body sensations (a headache, a fast heartbeat) are read as signs of cancer or heart disease. The person keeps checking the body, searching the internet and visiting doctors, or avoids them out of fear. In classical psychiatry a neurosis (psychoneurosis) is a disorder in which the person suffers distress but keeps insight and contact with reality, with no delusions or hallucinations. The neuroses listed in older texts include anxiety, phobic, obsessive-compulsive, hysterical, neurasthenic, depressive and hypochondriacal neurosis; DSM-II (1968) used the name 'hypochondriacal neurosis'. Psychosis, by contrast, is a loss of contact with reality. Example: Suresh is sure his headaches mean a brain tumour; three normal scans calm him for only a few days, yet he can admit the doctors may be right. Option b is right because the hypochondriac's worry is excessive but not delusional, so it belongs with the neuroses. Today DSM-5 (2013) divides it between somatic symptom disorder and illness anxiety disorder, while ICD-11 keeps the name hypochondriasis under obsessive-compulsive or related disorders. Exam trap: a fixed, unshakeable false belief of disease is a somatic delusion, which is psychotic (as in delusional disorder or psychotic depression); that is not hypochondria.", "topic": "Hypochondriasis as a neurosis", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Tendency to perceive objects as whole entities, despite the fact that some parts may be missing, is a law of:", "options": {"a": "Similarity", "b": "Proximity", "c": "Closure", "d": "Common region"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: the tendency to see objects as complete wholes even when parts are missing is the Gestalt law of closure. Max Wertheimer, Wolfgang Kohler and Kurt Koffka founded Gestalt psychology in Germany around 1912; they held that the whole is different from the sum of its parts, because the mind organises sensations into meaningful patterns. Wertheimer (1923) set out the laws of perceptual organisation. Closure: we fill in gaps, so a circle drawn with breaks, or the three corners of a triangle, are seen as a full circle or triangle. Gaetano Kanizsa's (1955) illusory triangle, made of three 'Pac-Man' shapes, makes us see a bright triangle that is not drawn at all. Similarity: items alike in colour or shape are grouped together. Proximity: items close together are grouped. Common region: items inside one boundary are grouped (a law added by Stephen Palmer, 1992). All fall under the law of Pragnanz (good form): we see the simplest, most stable organisation. Example: Riya passes a shop sign with one letter burnt out, 'SW_ETS', and still sees 'SWEETS'; her mind closes the gap. Option c is right because filling in missing parts to see a whole is exactly what closure means. Exam trap: continuity (good continuation), seeing smooth lines rather than sudden turns, is often confused with closure; closure completes gaps, continuity follows a path.", "topic": "Gestalt law of closure", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Cycle driving helps in scooter driving is transfer of learning:", "options": {"a": "Positive", "b": "Negative", "c": "Zero", "d": "None of the three"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: when cycling makes it easier to learn to ride a scooter, that is positive transfer of learning. Transfer of learning (transfer of training) is the effect of earlier learning on later learning. It is positive when the earlier learning helps the new task, negative when it interferes, and zero when it has no effect. Cycling and scooter riding share balance, steering, leaning into turns, braking and road sense, so a cyclist learns the scooter faster. Example: Arjun, who has cycled to school for years, rides his father's scooter confidently in two days, while his cousin, who never cycled, takes two weeks. Negative transfer would be a driver used to driving on the left in India struggling at first to drive on the right abroad. Theories of transfer include identical elements (Edward Thorndike and Robert Woodworth, 1901: transfer depends on elements the two tasks share); generalisation (Charles Judd, 1908: boys taught the principle of refraction hit an underwater target better when its depth changed); and transposition (the Gestalt view that relationships, not elements, transfer). Thorndike's work also undermined formal discipline, the belief that Latin or mathematics trains the mind as a whole. Option a is right because the earlier skill helps the new one. Exam trap: positive and negative here mean helping and hindering, not good and bad reinforcement; and bilateral transfer is a different term, meaning transfer from one hand or side of the body to the other.", "topic": "Positive transfer of learning", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Social distance scale is associated with:", "options": {"a": "Bogardus", "b": "Thurstone", "c": "Cattell", "d": "None of the three"}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a: the social distance scale was devised by Emory S. Bogardus (1925). Social distance is the degree of closeness a person is willing to accept with members of another group (ethnic, national, religious or caste). Bogardus listed seven graded relationships and asked respondents which they would accept for each group: to close kinship by marriage, to my club as personal chums, to my street as neighbours, to employment in my occupation, to citizenship in my country, as visitors only to my country, and would exclude from my country. The closest relationship a person accepts marks his social distance: the farther it is, the stronger the prejudice. It is often called the oldest attitude scale, and it is cumulative: accepting a close level (marriage) usually implies accepting the farther ones (neighbour, citizen). Example: Rahul would accept people of a certain community as co-workers and fellow citizens but not as neighbours or relatives; his answers mark a moderate social distance. Option a is right because the social distance scale is Bogardus's own scale. Exam trap: Louis Thurstone (with Ernest Chave, 1929) built a different attitude scale, the method of equal-appearing intervals, in which judges sort statements into 11 piles; examiners pair the two names because both built classic attitude scales.", "topic": "Bogardus social distance scale", "fig": "Emory S. Bogardus"}, {"q": "Rapport development is necessarily needed for:", "options": {"a": "Teacher", "b": "Doctor", "c": "Manager", "d": "Counsellor"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: rapport is necessarily needed for a counsellor, because counselling works through the relationship itself. Rapport is a warm, trusting, comfortable working relationship in which the client feels safe, accepted and understood. In counselling it is the first step and the foundation of everything that follows: without it a client will not reveal fears, failures or family secrets, and no assessment or advice can work. Counsellors build it by greeting warmly, explaining the purpose and the limits of confidentiality, listening closely, starting with neutral topics and never judging. Carl Rogers (1957) set out the conditions he held to be necessary and sufficient for therapeutic change; the three that come from the counsellor, now called the core conditions, are congruence (genuineness), unconditional positive regard and empathic understanding. Example: Pooja, 17, sent to the school counsellor for falling marks, says little at the first meeting; only after the counsellor has listened without blame for two sessions does she reveal that her parents are separating. Option d is right because the counsellor's whole method is the helping relationship, so rapport is essential, not optional. Exam trap: teachers, doctors and managers also work better with rapport, so the other options feel reasonable; but only for the counsellor is it a necessary condition of the work itself. A surgeon can operate well with little rapport; a counsellor cannot counsel without it.", "topic": "Rapport in counselling", "fig": ""}, {"q": "TAT and RIB are ______ personality assessment techniques.", "options": {"a": "Subjective", "b": "Objective", "c": "Projective", "d": "All the three"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: the Thematic Apperception Test (TAT) and the Rorschach Ink Blot test (RIB) are projective techniques of personality assessment. Projective techniques present ambiguous, unstructured stimuli and ask the person to describe them or tell a story; because the material has no fixed meaning, the person projects his own needs, conflicts, motives and feelings onto it. Lawrence Frank (1939) coined the term 'projective methods'. The TAT, by Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray (1935), has 31 picture cards (one of them blank); Henry Murray's standard procedure gave 20 over two sessions, though many clinicians now use fewer, and the person tells a story for each: what led up to the scene, what is happening, what the characters feel and how it ends. The stories are analysed for needs (such as achievement) and press (forces from the environment). The Rorschach, by Hermann Rorschach (1921, Psychodiagnostik), uses 10 symmetrical inkblots; responses are scored for location, determinants (form, colour, movement, shading) and content, now often with John Exner's (1974) Comprehensive System. Example: shown the TAT card of a boy gazing at a violin, Vikram tells of a boy forced by his father to practise who secretly dreams of running away; the psychologist notes themes of parental pressure and escape. Option c is right because both tests use ambiguous stimuli to draw out hidden aspects of personality. Exam trap: objective tests are structured self-report inventories with fixed answers and objective scoring (MMPI, 16PF, Eysenck Personality Inventory), the opposite of the TAT and Rorschach.", "topic": "Projective tests: TAT and Rorschach", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In Psychosis a person has ______ contact with reality.", "options": {"a": "Some", "b": "No", "c": "Full", "d": "None of the three"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: in psychosis a person has no contact with reality; loss of reality contact is what separates psychosis from neurosis. Psychosis is a severe mental disorder in which reality testing, the ability to tell inner experience from the outside world, breaks down. Its signs are hallucinations (perceptions without a stimulus), delusions (fixed false beliefs), disorganised thinking and speech, and little or no insight: the person does not see that he is ill. It occurs in schizophrenia, delusional disorder, mania or severe depression with psychotic features, and some organic and drug-induced states. Neurosis is the contrast: the person suffers anxiety, phobias or obsessions but keeps contact with reality and knows the fears are excessive. The standard textbook table reads: neurosis, contact kept and insight present; psychosis, contact lost and insight absent; neurosis is usually treated with counselling or psychotherapy, psychosis usually with medication and sometimes hospital care. Example: Ramesh believes his neighbours have hidden cameras in his ceiling fan and hears them commenting on him; no argument shakes the belief, and he refuses treatment because he says nothing is wrong with him. Option b is right because loss of contact with reality is the defining mark of psychosis. Exam trap: 'full' contact describes the normal person and, broadly, the neurotic. Key note: many people with psychosis keep partial contact (they may know where they are and talk normally while holding a delusion), so 'some' is arguable; but the textbook contrast is that neurosis keeps contact and psychosis loses it, so the answer used here, b, stands.", "topic": "Psychosis and reality contact", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Parental attention to their crying child is:", "options": {"a": "Voluntary", "b": "Involuntary", "c": "Both (A) and (B)", "d": "None of the three"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: a parent's attention to a crying child is involuntary attention, captured at once without any deliberate effort. Attention is the focusing of consciousness on one object out of many. William James (1890) wrote that attention is either 'passive, reflex, non-voluntary, effortless' or 'active and voluntary', and Indian educational psychology texts keep this two-way split. Voluntary (volitional) attention is deliberate and effortful: we direct it by will toward a goal, as when a student forces himself to concentrate on a dull chapter before an exam; it tires us and needs motivation. Involuntary (non-voluntary) attention is captured without effort or decision, either by the stimulus itself (a loud bang, sudden movement, intense colour, novelty) or by our instincts, emotions and interests. A baby's cry is both: a loud, urgent sound and a signal that stirs the parental instinct, which William McDougall (1908) listed, with its emotion of tenderness, among the human instincts. Example: Sunita is cooking and talking on the phone; the moment her baby cries in the next room, her attention swings to him before she has decided anything. Option b is right because the cry pulls the parent's attention automatically, through stimulus intensity and instinct, not by an act of will. Exam trap: 'voluntary' is tempting because a caring parent wants to attend; but wanting is not effort, and voluntary attention is the effortful kind a student needs for a boring lesson. Key note: a parent can also choose to keep attending, which looks voluntary, and some texts split involuntary (non-volitional) attention into 'enforced' (driven by instinct) and 'spontaneous' (driven by sentiment or interest), so the labels vary; but in the voluntary versus involuntary split the instinct-driven pull of a baby's cry is involuntary, so the answer used here, b, stands.", "topic": "Voluntary and involuntary attention", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Two-factor theory of Intelligence is associated with:", "options": {"a": "Guilford", "b": "Thorndike", "c": "Spearman", "d": "Thurstone"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: the two-factor theory of intelligence is associated with Charles Spearman. Charles Spearman (1904), a British psychologist, noticed that schoolchildren's marks in very different areas (classics, mathematics, English, even pitch discrimination) all correlated positively: a child good at one tended to be good at the others. He explained this positive manifold with two kinds of factor. The general factor, g, is a common mental energy that enters every intellectual task. The specific factors, s, are narrow abilities, each peculiar to one task. So every test score is made of g plus its own s, and tests correlate with each other because they share g. To show this he developed an early form of factor analysis, and he set out the theory fully in The Abilities of Man (1927). Example: Riya scores high in arithmetic, vocabulary and puzzles; Spearman would credit her strong g for all three, and her own s for the extra edge she shows in arithmetic. Option c is right because 'two-factor' means exactly these two factors, g and s, and the theory carries Spearman's name. Exam trap: the label 'two-factor theory' is reused elsewhere, for Stanley Schachter and Jerome Singer's (1962) theory of emotion, O. Hobart Mowrer's (1947) theory of avoidance learning and Frederick Herzberg's (1959) theory of work motivation; for intelligence it is always Spearman. Examiners also set the multiple-factor theorists beside him: Louis Thurstone (1938) found seven primary abilities, J. P. Guilford (1967) proposed 120, and Edward Thorndike (1920) split intelligence into abstract, mechanical and social.", "topic": "Spearman's two-factor theory", "fig": "Charles Spearman"}, {"q": "A prejudiced person is:", "options": {"a": "Biased", "b": "Blind to reason", "c": "Both (A) and (B)", "d": "Impartial"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: a prejudiced person is both biased and blind to reason. Prejudice literally means pre-judgement (Latin prae, before, and judicium, judgement). It is an attitude, usually negative, toward a group and its members, formed before or without adequate evidence and held simply because of group membership. Gordon Allport (1954), in The Nature of Prejudice, called ethnic prejudice 'an antipathy based upon a faulty and inflexible generalization'. Both halves of that phrase match the options. 'Faulty generalization' is bias: the person prejudges every member of a group by a stereotype. 'Inflexible' is blindness to reason: Allport noted that prejudgements become prejudices only when they are not reversible when exposed to new knowledge. Prejudice has three components: cognitive (the stereotype), affective (the dislike) and behavioural (discrimination). Example: Arjun believes all people from a certain region are lazy (bias). He then works beside Kabir from that region, who works harder than anyone in the office, and simply decides Kabir is an exception, keeping his belief intact (blind to reason). Allport called this trick of admitting exceptions while keeping the generalisation 're-fencing'. Option c is right because prejudice needs both the slanted prejudgement and the refusal to revise it in the face of evidence. Exam trap: examiners split the definition into two options so that a student who remembers only one half picks it alone; also keep prejudice (an attitude) apart from discrimination (the action) and a stereotype (the belief).", "topic": "Prejudice: definition", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Rogers is associated with ______ counselling.", "options": {"a": "Directive", "b": "Non-directive", "c": "Psychoanalytic", "d": "All the three"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: Carl Rogers is associated with non-directive counselling. Carl Rogers (1942), in Counseling and Psychotherapy, described a method in which the counsellor does not advise, diagnose, interpret or steer. The client leads, choosing what to talk about, and the counsellor listens, accepts and reflects back the feelings the client expresses, so that the client gains insight and reaches his own decisions. Rogers trusted an inborn actualising tendency: given the right relationship, people grow on their own. He later renamed the approach client-centred (Client-Centered Therapy, 1951) and then person-centred, and in 1957 he named its core conditions: genuineness (congruence), unconditional positive regard and empathic understanding. Example: Meera tells the school counsellor she is confused about leaving the science stream. A non-directive counsellor says, 'You feel torn between what your parents want and what you want,' rather than 'Take commerce.' Option b is right because Rogers' method was named non-directive precisely because the counsellor gives up direction and the responsibility for choices stays with the client. Exam trap: the paired name is Edmund G. Williamson (1939), the Minnesota trait-factor counsellor whose directive, counsellor-centred counselling runs through analysis, synthesis, diagnosis, prognosis, counselling and follow-up. Directive = Williamson; non-directive = Rogers; eclectic = Frederick Thorne (1950), who combined the two as the case required.", "topic": "Rogers' non-directive counselling", "fig": "Carl Rogers"}, {"q": "Cattell's Test of Personality has ______ factors.", "options": {"a": "10", "b": "8", "c": "12", "d": "16"}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: Cattell's personality test, the Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire (16PF), measures 16 factors. Raymond Cattell (1949) built it by factor analysis. He began from the lexical idea that important traits are coded in everyday language, took the list of about 18,000 trait words compiled by Gordon Allport and Henry Odbert (1936), cut it down, and factor-analysed ratings and questionnaire answers. Clusters of traits that simply go together he called surface traits; the deeper factors underneath them he called source traits, and 16 of these form the 16PF. They are labelled by letters: A warmth, B reasoning, C emotional stability, E dominance, F liveliness, G rule-consciousness, H social boldness, I sensitivity, L vigilance, M abstractedness, N privateness, O apprehension, and Q1 to Q4 (openness to change, self-reliance, perfectionism, tension). Scores are reported as stens (1 to 10), and the fifth edition (1993) has 185 items. Example: Kabir's 16PF profile shows high H (socially bold) and high Q2 (self-reliant), which his career counsellor uses when discussing jobs. Option d is right because sixteen source traits are the defining feature of the test, as its very name says. Exam trap: other personality measures carry other numbers, and examiners mix them up: Hans Eysenck's (1975) Eysenck Personality Questionnaire has three superfactors (psychoticism, extraversion, neuroticism), the Big Five has five, and Cattell's own High School Personality Questionnaire for adolescents measures 14 factors.", "topic": "Cattell's 16PF", "fig": "Raymond Cattell"}, {"q": "Paranoid personality disorder is characterised by:", "options": {"a": "Pervasive distrust", "b": "Suspiciousness of others", "c": "Both (A) and (B)", "d": "None of the three"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: paranoid personality disorder is marked by both pervasive distrust and suspiciousness of others. DSM-5-TR (2022) defines it as 'a pervasive distrust and suspiciousness of others such that their motives are interpreted as malevolent', beginning by early adulthood and present in many settings. At least four of seven signs are needed: suspecting, without good reason, that others are exploiting, harming or deceiving him; doubts about the loyalty of friends; reluctance to confide for fear the information will be used against him; reading hidden insults or threats into harmless remarks; bearing grudges; seeing attacks on his character that others do not see and counterattacking quickly; and repeated unjustified suspicion that a spouse is unfaithful. It belongs to Cluster A (odd or eccentric), with schizoid and schizotypal personality disorders. Unlike delusional disorder or schizophrenia, the suspicions do not reach the level of fixed delusions and there are no hallucinations. Example: Vikram is sure his colleagues are plotting to get him sacked, will not tell a close friend his salary in case it is used against him, and still holds a grudge over a joke made years ago. Option c is right because the core criterion joins the two words: distrust and suspiciousness are both named in the same defining sentence. Exam trap: examiners offer one word of the pair as a separate option; also do not confuse paranoid personality (a lasting trait pattern without delusions) with delusional disorder, persecutory type, or with the old paranoid subtype of schizophrenia (dropped in DSM-5).", "topic": "Paranoid personality disorder", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Median is a measure of:", "options": {"a": "Central tendency", "b": "Variability", "c": "Prediction", "d": "All the three"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: the median is a measure of central tendency. A measure of central tendency is a single score that represents the centre, the typical value, of a set of scores. There are three: the mean (the arithmetic average), the median and the mode (the most frequent score). The median is the middle score when all the scores are arranged in order: half the group lies above it and half below, so it is also the 50th percentile and the second quartile (Q2). With an odd number of scores it is the middle one; with an even number it is the average of the two middle ones. Its great strength is that extreme scores do not pull it, so it is preferred for skewed data such as income, and for open-ended classes. Example: Riya's group of five scores 3, 5, 7, 9 and 40 on a quiz; the median is 7, but the mean is 12.8, dragged up by the one very high score, so the median describes the typical student better. Option a is right because the median locates the centre of a distribution, not its spread and not a forecast. Exam trap: the median is often confused with the quartile deviation, which uses Q1 and Q3 to measure spread; both come from the same ordered list of scores, but only the median (Q2) is a measure of central tendency.", "topic": "Median: central tendency", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Convergent thinking is same as:", "options": {"a": "Creativity", "b": "Intelligence", "c": "Both (A) and (B)", "d": "None of the three"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: convergent thinking is the kind of thinking intelligence tests measure, so of these options it matches intelligence. J. P. Guilford (1950), in his presidential address to the American Psychological Association, and later in his Structure of Intellect model (1967), separated two mental operations. Convergent production (convergent thinking) narrows information down to the single correct answer, as in an IQ test item, a maths sum or a multiple-choice question. Divergent production (divergent thinking) spreads out to many varied, original answers to an open problem; it is the core of creativity and is scored for fluency (how many ideas), flexibility (how many kinds), originality (how rare) and elaboration (how detailed). It is tested by tasks such as listing all the uses of a brick and by the Tests of Creative Thinking of E. Paul Torrance (1966). Example: asked 'What is 7 times 8?', Arjun converges on 56; asked 'How many uses can you think of for a newspaper?', he diverges: wrapping, a fan, a paper boat, a hat. Option b is right because standard intelligence tests reward convergent thinking, while creativity rests on divergent thinking. Exam trap: students swap the pair; remember that convergent means coming together on one answer (intelligence) and divergent means spreading out to many answers (creativity). Key note: convergent thinking is only one part of intelligence, not literally the same thing, but of the options intelligence is its match while creativity is divergent thinking, so b stands.", "topic": "Convergent vs divergent thinking", "fig": "J. P. Guilford"}, {"q": "The most useful communication for Blind Children is:", "options": {"a": "Non-verbal", "b": "Verbal", "c": "Both (A) and (B)", "d": "None of the three"}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: verbal communication is the most useful channel for blind children. Communication is either verbal (words, spoken or written) or non-verbal (everything else: facial expression, gesture, eye contact, posture, distance, touch and tone of voice). Most non-verbal cues are visual, so a child who cannot see misses a smile, a nod, a frown or a pointing finger. Spoken language and careful listening therefore carry most of a blind child's learning and social contact, and Braille, the raised-dot script devised by Louis Braille (1824), gives a verbal code for reading and writing through the fingertips. Teachers of visually impaired children are trained to describe actions aloud, call the child by name before speaking, say who has entered the room, and give spoken feedback instead of a nod. Example: in Meera's class the teacher says, 'Meera, the map is on your desk; India is under your right hand,' rather than pointing at it. Option b is right because words reach a blind child fully, while most non-verbal signals do not reach him at all. Exam trap: 'non-verbal' sounds gentler and more natural, so it tempts; for deaf children the stress does shift to visual channels such as sign and lip-reading, but for blind children it is verbal. Key note: touch is non-verbal and does matter for blind children, so a or c has a case; but most non-verbal channels are visual and lost to them, and language is their main channel, so b stands.", "topic": "Communication with blind children", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Teacher is to student as Counsellor is to:", "options": {"a": "Counselee", "b": "Client", "c": "Both (A) and (B)", "d": "None of the three"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: the person a counsellor helps is called both a counselee and a client. The analogy works like this: a teacher teaches a student, and a counsellor counsels a counselee, also called a client. Both terms are standard. 'Counselee' (the one who is counselled) is the usual word in school guidance texts, especially in accounts of directive counselling, the approach of Edmund G. Williamson (1939) in which the counsellor, rather like a teacher, collects data, diagnoses the problem and advises. 'Client' was made standard by Carl Rogers (1951), whose approach was named client-centred therapy: he preferred it to 'patient' to stress that the person is an active, responsible adult who seeks help by choice, not a sick person waiting to be treated. Indian guidance and counselling texts use the two words interchangeably for the same person. Example: when Kabir, a Class 12 pupil, meets the school counsellor about choosing a career, the books may call him the counselee or the client; the meaning is the same. Option c is right because both words name the person on the receiving end of counselling, just as 'student' names the person on the receiving end of teaching. Exam trap: examiners split two synonyms into separate options so that a student who knows only one of them picks it alone; a third word, 'patient', belongs to medicine and psychiatry and is avoided in counselling.", "topic": "Counselee and client", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Perfect correlation is:", "options": {"a": "0.9", "b": "0.8", "c": "1.0", "d": "0.5"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: perfect correlation is 1.0 (that is, 1.00). The correlation coefficient tells how closely two variables move together. Karl Pearson's (1896) product-moment coefficient, r, runs from minus 1.00 through 0 to plus 1.00. The sign shows direction: plus means both rise together (height and weight), minus means one rises as the other falls (days absent and marks). The size, ignoring the sign, shows strength. A perfect correlation is r = plus 1.00 (perfect positive) or minus 1.00 (perfect negative): every point on the scatterplot lies exactly on a straight line, so one variable predicts the other without error. Zero means no straight-line relationship. As a rough guide, 0.9 is very high, 0.8 high and 0.5 moderate; real psychological data are almost never perfect. Example: if Riya is paid exactly 100 rupees for every hour she works, her hours and her pay correlate plus 1.00; her hours of study and her exam marks might correlate about 0.5, because many other things affect marks. Option c is right because only 1.00 means the relationship holds without a single exception. Exam trap: a very high r such as 0.9 tempts students, but high is not perfect; and minus 1.00 is just as perfect as plus 1.00, because the sign shows only direction, not strength. Key note: the paper prints '01', a slip for 1 (that is, 1.00); read that way c stands, and perfect negative correlation is minus 1.00.", "topic": "Perfect correlation", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Mentally retarded is/are:", "options": {"a": "Morons", "b": "Imbeciles", "c": "Idiots", "d": "All the three"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: morons, imbeciles and idiots were all old labels for levels of mental retardation. Early in the twentieth century, mental deficiency was graded by severity, roughly by IQ: idiot (IQ below about 25, needing total lifelong care), imbecile (about 25 to 50, able to learn self-care and simple tasks under supervision) and moron (about 50 to 70, able to learn basic school skills and simple work). Henry Goddard (1910) coined 'moron' from a Greek word meaning foolish. Over time all three words turned into insults and were dropped. They were replaced first by mild, moderate, severe and profound mental retardation (in DSM-IV, mild ran from an IQ of 50 to 55 up to about 70), and then by intellectual disability (intellectual developmental disorder) in DSM-5 (2013), where severity is judged by adaptive functioning, not by IQ alone; ICD-11 uses disorders of intellectual development. Schools also used the labels educable, trainable and custodial. Example: an old hospital record describing Kabir's great-uncle as an 'imbecile' would today read moderate intellectual disability. Option d is right because each word named one band of the same condition, from mildest (moron) to most severe (idiot). Exam trap: students pick moron alone because it is the best-known word, or think idiot and imbecile are everyday insults rather than technical grades; all three were levels on one scale. Learn the old terms only to recognise them in exams, and never use them with clients or families.", "topic": "Old mental retardation labels", "fig": ""}, {"q": "SD in ungrouped data is computed by:", "options": {"a": "Σx² / N", "b": "√(Σx² / N)", "c": "√Σx / N", "d": "None of the three"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: for ungrouped data the standard deviation is SD = √(Σx² / N), where x = X minus M, the deviation of each score from the mean. The SD tells how far scores typically lie from the mean; it is the most widely used measure of variability, and Karl Pearson (1894) gave it its name. The steps follow the formula from the inside out. Find the mean (M). Subtract it from each score to get x. Square each x, which removes the minus signs and gives extra weight to large deviations. Add the squares to get Σx². Divide by N, which gives the variance, the mean of the squared deviations. Finally take the square root, which brings the answer back to the original units of measurement. Example: Meera's three test scores are 2, 4 and 6. The mean is 4; the deviations are minus 2, 0 and plus 2; the squares are 4, 0 and 4; Σx² = 8; 8 divided by 3 = 2.67, the variance; the square root of 2.67 = 1.63, the SD. When a sample is used to estimate the population SD, many books divide by N minus 1 instead of N. Option b is right because the SD is the square root of the variance, and only option b takes that root. Exam trap: option a, Σx² / N, is a real formula, but it is the variance (the SD squared), so students who forget the final square root pick it.", "topic": "SD formula, ungrouped data", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Psychoanalysis is related to:", "options": {"a": "Id", "b": "Ego", "c": "Super Ego", "d": "All the three"}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: the id, the ego and the superego are all concepts of psychoanalysis. Psychoanalysis is both the theory of personality and the method of therapy founded by Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939). In The Ego and the Id (1923) he proposed his structural model of the mind, with three systems. The id is present at birth and wholly unconscious, the store of instinctual drives (the life instincts, including sex, and the death or aggressive instincts); it works on the pleasure principle, seeking instant gratification through primary process thinking, the wish-fulfilling imagery seen in dreams. The ego grows out of the id to deal with the real world; it works on the reality principle and secondary process (realistic, logical) thinking, mediates between id, superego and reality, and uses defence mechanisms when anxiety rises. The superego is the internalised voice of parents and society, formed around age 5 as the Oedipus complex resolves; its two parts are the conscience, which punishes with guilt, and the ego ideal, which rewards with pride. Example: hungry Arjun sees a classmate's tiffin; his id says 'grab it', his superego says 'stealing is wrong', and his ego decides to ask politely for a share. Option d is right because all three structures belong to the same psychoanalytic model. Exam trap: the earlier topographic model (1900: conscious, preconscious, unconscious) is a different Freudian model, and examiners also swap the principles: pleasure = id, reality = ego, morality = superego.", "topic": "Freud's structural model", "fig": "Sigmund Freud"}, {"q": "Air pollution is highest in:", "options": {"a": "Habitations", "b": "Villages", "c": "Towns", "d": "Cities"}, "correct": "d", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is d: outdoor air pollution is highest in cities. Air pollution is the presence in the air of harmful gases and particles: fine particulate matter (PM2.5 and PM10), nitrogen dioxide, sulphur dioxide, carbon monoxide and ground-level ozone. Cities concentrate every major source in a small area: heavy vehicle traffic, factories and power plants, construction and road dust, burning of waste, and millions of people living close together, often among tall buildings that trap still air. So pollution rises along the settlement ladder: small habitations and villages have few sources and plenty of open space, towns have more, and cities the most. Data from the World Health Organization and the IQAir rankings regularly place Indian cities such as Delhi among the most polluted in the world, especially in winter, when smoke from crop-stubble burning in neighbouring states adds to the city's own load. Environmental psychology links poor air not only to lung and heart disease but also to lower mood, poorer attention and thinking, and more irritability and aggression. Example: Kabir moves from his village in Punjab to Delhi for college and notices burning eyes and a grey haze every November. Option d is right because cities have the densest concentration of pollution sources. Exam trap: the options run from the smallest settlement to the largest, and examiners hope students will think of rural smoke; outdoor pollution grows with size, so the largest, the city, is the answer. Key note: the paper prints 'Air population', a slip for air pollution. Indoor smoke from cooking fires is a serious rural problem, but outdoor air pollution is highest in cities, and if the word meant population, cities are highest too, so d stands either way.", "topic": "Air pollution and settlements", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Remedial classes are needed for:", "options": {"a": "Gifted", "b": "Underachievers", "c": "Intelligent", "d": "Both (A) and (C)"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: remedial classes are needed for underachievers. Remedial teaching is special instruction that finds and corrects specific weaknesses in pupils whose achievement falls below their ability or below the level of their class. An underachiever is a pupil whose actual achievement is clearly lower than his measured ability (IQ or aptitude) predicts: the potential is there, but something blocks it, such as gaps in basic skills, poor study habits, a specific learning disability, low motivation, anxiety or problems at home. Remedial work follows a sequence: a diagnostic test pinpoints exactly what the child gets wrong (which kinds of subtraction, which letter sounds), targeted teaching in small groups then fixes those gaps, and a re-test checks progress. Slow learners and children with learning disabilities also receive remedial help. Gifted and highly intelligent pupils need the opposite kind of provision: enrichment (deeper, broader work), acceleration (moving ahead faster) or special programmes, because ordinary lessons are too easy for them. Example: Riya has an IQ of 115 but keeps failing maths because she never understood place value; after a diagnostic test, remedial lessons on place value lift her marks to match her ability. Option b is right because remedial classes exist to close the gap between ability and achievement, and that gap is the definition of underachievement. Exam trap: a gifted pupil can also underachieve (a gifted underachiever), and then remedial help is needed for the underachievement; the label 'gifted' on its own calls for enrichment, not remediation.", "topic": "Remedial teaching and underachievers", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Creativity and Intelligence have a ______ relationship.", "options": {"a": "Positive", "b": "Perfect", "c": "Negative", "d": "None of the three"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: creativity and intelligence have a positive relationship, though a modest one. Intelligence tests reward convergent thinking (one right answer), while creativity tests reward divergent thinking (many original answers), a split drawn by J. P. Guilford (1950). If the two were the same ability they would correlate almost perfectly; if they were opposed, the correlation would be negative. Research finds neither. Kyung Hee Kim (2005), pooling many studies of creativity test scores and IQ in a meta-analysis, found an average correlation of about 0.17: positive, but small, so IQ accounts for only about 3% of the differences in creativity scores. The threshold hypothesis adds that the two rise together up to an IQ of about 120, and that above that level extra intelligence adds little to creativity. Jacob Getzels and Philip Jackson (1962) found that highly creative and highly intelligent adolescents were largely different groups, and Michael Wallach and Nathan Kogan (1965) found the two nearly independent when creativity tests were given in a relaxed, game-like way. So some intelligence helps creativity, but a high IQ does not guarantee it. Example: Arjun (IQ 135) solves puzzles quickly but writes ordinary stories, while Meera (IQ 112) produces the most original ideas in her class. Option a is right because the overall correlation is above zero. Exam trap: 'positive' does not mean 'strong'; a small positive correlation is still positive, and 'perfect' would mean the two abilities are identical. Key note: the link is weak, and close to zero above about IQ 120, so some answer d; but the overall correlation is positive (it is neither perfect nor negative), so a stands.", "topic": "Creativity and intelligence", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Delinquents:", "options": {"a": "maintain social norms", "b": "violate social norms", "c": "wander", "d": "both (B) and (C)"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: delinquents violate social norms. Juvenile delinquency is behaviour by a minor that breaks the law or goes against the accepted norms of society: theft, vandalism, fighting, drug use and, in some legal systems, status offences such as truancy or running away from home, which are offences only because of the child's age. In India the Juvenile Justice (Care and Protection of Children) Act, 2015 calls a person under 18 who is alleged to have committed an offence a 'child in conflict with law' and his case goes first to a Juvenile Justice Board, not an ordinary criminal court. Sociologists explain delinquency in several ways: the strain (anomie) theory of Robert Merton (1938), in which blocked legitimate goals push youth toward illegitimate means; the differential association theory of Edwin Sutherland (1939), in which delinquency is learned from close associates; and the delinquent subculture of Albert Cohen (1955). The clinical counterpart is conduct disorder, a repetitive pattern of violating the rights of others or major age-appropriate norms. Example: Rahul, 15, steals mobile phones from a market with older friends; his acts break both the law and the norms of his community. Option b is right because norm violation is the defining feature shared by every delinquent act. Exam trap: maintaining norms is conformity, the opposite; and wandering is only one behaviour some delinquents show, not the definition. Key note: some texts list vagrancy and running away from home as delinquent acts, so d is tempting; but the defining feature, true of every delinquent, is violating social norms, so b stands.", "topic": "Juvenile delinquency", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Sensation is related to:", "options": {"a": "Skin", "b": "Eyes and ears", "c": "Nose and tongue", "d": "All the three"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: sensation is related to the skin, the eyes and ears, and the nose and tongue, all three. Sensation is the process by which sense organs detect physical energy from the world or from the body and convert it into nerve impulses (transduction) that travel to the brain; perception is the next step, in which the brain organises and interprets those signals. Each sense has its own receptors: the eyes (rods and cones in the retina) respond to light, giving vision; the ears (hair cells in the cochlea) respond to sound waves, giving hearing; the nose (olfactory receptors) responds to airborne chemicals, giving smell; the tongue (taste buds) responds to dissolved chemicals, giving taste; and the skin holds receptors for touch, pressure, warmth, cold and pain. These are the five classic senses, and psychology adds the kinaesthetic sense (body position and movement, from receptors in muscles and joints) and the vestibular sense (balance, from the inner ear). Example: at a wedding, Meera sees the lights (eyes), hears the band (ears), smells the jasmine (nose), tastes the sweets (tongue) and feels the warmth of a diya near her hand (skin): five sensations at once. Option d is right because every listed organ houses sensory receptors. Exam trap: students sometimes count only the 'higher' senses of sight and hearing, or think of the skin as a mere covering; the skin is the largest sense organ and carries several separate senses.", "topic": "Sense organs and sensation", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Self-actualisation is associated with:", "options": {"a": "Rogers", "b": "Maslow", "c": "Freud", "d": "None of the three"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: self-actualisation is associated with Abraham Maslow. Abraham Maslow (1943), in 'A Theory of Human Motivation', arranged human needs in a hierarchy: physiological, safety, love and belongingness, esteem, and at the top self-actualisation, the need to become everything one is capable of becoming. In his words, 'What a man can be, he must be.' The lower (deficiency) needs must be fairly well met before the growth need of self-actualisation drives behaviour. In Motivation and Personality (1954) he described self-actualisers, a small, hand-picked group including Abraham Lincoln, Albert Einstein and Eleanor Roosevelt: realistic, accepting, spontaneous, problem-centred and independent, and prone to peak experiences, moments of intense joy and awe. He called humanistic psychology the 'third force', after psychoanalysis and behaviourism. Example: Kabir has a secure job and a loving family, yet feels restless until he starts teaching music to village children, the work he feels he was born to do; that is self-actualisation. Option b is right because self-actualisation is the peak of Maslow's hierarchy and the concept most tied to his name. Exam trap: Kurt Goldstein (1939) coined the term, and Carl Rogers (1951) built his theory on an actualising tendency; examiners use both to pull students away from Maslow. Key note: Kurt Goldstein coined the term and Carl Rogers built his theory on the actualising tendency, so a has a case; but the name tied to self-actualisation in textbooks is Maslow's, at the top of his hierarchy, so b stands.", "topic": "Maslow's self-actualisation", "fig": "Abraham Maslow"}, {"q": "Social Loafing is associated with:", "options": {"a": "Individual Projects", "b": "Group Projects", "c": "Both (A) and (B)", "d": "None of the three"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: social loafing is associated with group projects. Social loafing is the tendency of people to put in less effort when they work together on a group task, with their contributions pooled so that no one's share can be seen, than when they work alone. Max Ringelmann (1913), a French agricultural engineer, first noticed it: when men pulled on a rope, the force each one exerted fell as the team grew. Part of the loss was poor coordination, but Bibb Latané, Kipling Williams and Stephen Harkins (1979) showed that motivation falls too: students asked to shout and clap as loudly as they could made less noise each as the group grew, even when they only believed others were shouting with them. Steven Karau and Kipling Williams (1993) reviewed the studies in a meta-analysis: loafing shrinks when each person's work can be identified and evaluated, when the task matters, and when the group is cohesive, and it is weaker in collectivist cultures and among women. Example: in a four-member college project, Arjun does almost nothing because only one group mark will be given; if each member's section were graded separately, he would work harder. Option b is right because loafing needs a group task with pooled, unidentifiable effort. Exam trap: do not confuse social loafing with social facilitation (Norman Triplett, 1898; Robert Zajonc, 1965), in which the mere presence of others, with each person's performance visible, speeds up well-learned tasks.", "topic": "Social loafing", "fig": ""}]};
window.PREVIEW_DEEP = Object.assign(window.PREVIEW_DEEP||{}, {"jammu-psy": [{"q": "Guidance means:", "concept": "The answer is b: guidance means assistance, help offered to a person so that he can understand himself, make his own choices and solve his own problems. Lester D. Crow and Alice Crow (1951), in An Introduction to Guidance, stressed that guidance is not giving directions and not imposing one person's point of view on another. It is help given by trained people to a person of any age so that he can manage his own life, form his own views, make his own decisions and carry his own burdens. The aim is self-direction: a well-guided person needs less guidance over time. Guidance works by giving the person the facts he needs (about his abilities, his interests and the options open to him) and helping him weigh them. Example: Arjun, in Class 10, cannot decide between science and commerce. A counsellor gives him an aptitude test, shares information on courses and careers and talks through his interests; Arjun then makes the choice himself. If the counsellor simply said 'take science', that would be direction, not guidance. Option b is right because guidance is assistance that leaves the decision with the person being helped. Exam trap: in everyday speech 'guidance' sounds like advice or instruction, so a or c looks tempting; but guidance textbooks define it against both, since advice and direction keep the decision with the helper.", "traps": {"a": "Advice is telling a person what you think he should do; guidance texts say guidance is more than advice, because it helps the person reach his own decision rather than accept the helper's.", "c": "Direction means instructing a person what to do; Crow and Crow say plainly that guidance is not giving directions, since that takes the decision away from the person.", "d": "None of the three fails because assistance (b) is the textbook meaning of guidance."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a probability sample?", "concept": "The answer is c: both quota and purposive sampling are non-probability samples. In probability sampling every member of the population has a known, non-zero chance of being chosen, and chance alone decides who is picked, so sampling error can be estimated. Its forms are simple random, systematic, stratified random, cluster and multistage sampling. In non-probability sampling the researcher's judgement or convenience decides who gets in, so some members have no chance at all and the results cannot be safely generalised. Quota sampling fixes how many people to take from each category (say 50 men and 50 women), much like stratified sampling, but the interviewer then picks whoever is handy within each quota. Purposive (judgemental) sampling means the researcher hand-picks cases he thinks typical or informative. Example: Meera wants to study teachers' stress. If she numbers all 500 teachers in her district and draws 100 by lottery, that is a random (probability) sample. If she interviews the first 20 male and 20 female teachers she meets, that is quota; if she chooses ten teachers she knows to be very experienced, that is purposive. Option c is right because neither quota nor purposive selection gives each member a known chance of being chosen. Exam trap: quota sampling looks like stratified random sampling, since both divide the population into categories; the difference is that stratified sampling draws randomly within each stratum, while quota sampling lets the interviewer choose.", "traps": {"a": "Quota sampling is indeed non-probability (fixed numbers per category, but the interviewer chooses who fills them); picking it alone misses that purposive sampling is non-probability too.", "b": "Purposive (judgemental) sampling is indeed non-probability because the researcher hand-picks the cases; picking it alone misses that quota sampling is non-probability too.", "d": "Random sampling is the textbook probability method, giving every member an equal, known chance of selection, so it is the one option that is a probability sample."}}, {"q": "Abnormality is characterised by ______ deviation.", "concept": "The answer is c: abnormality, in the statistical sense, is deviation from the average in either direction, positive or negative. The statistical criterion defines normal as what is common and abnormal as what is rare. Many human traits, such as intelligence, height or anxiety, follow the normal (bell-shaped) curve: most people cluster near the mean, and fewer appear as you move out towards either tail. Anyone far from the mean, at either end, is statistically abnormal. Example: on IQ tests with a mean of 100 and an SD of 15, about 2% of people score below 70 and about 2% score above 130. Kabir, with an IQ of 65, and Riya, with an IQ of 145, are both statistically abnormal, though only Kabir's deviation is a problem. Option c is right because the deviation view of abnormality measures distance from the norm on both sides. The weakness of this criterion shows in Riya's case: it labels desirable rarities (genius, unusual calm, great musical talent) abnormal. So abnormal psychology adds other criteria, often summed up as the four Ds: deviance from social norms, distress, dysfunction (maladaptive behaviour) and danger. Exam trap: because 'abnormal' sounds bad, students pick negative deviation; but the statistical idea counts rarity, not harm, so both tails are abnormal. Key note: clinically only harmful deviations count as a disorder, so some would argue for a; but the deviation view of abnormality that the stem uses runs both ways from the norm, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Negative deviation (below the average, as in intellectual disability) is only the lower tail of the curve; the statistical criterion also counts deviation above the mean.", "b": "Positive deviation (above the average, as in giftedness) is only the upper tail; picking it alone ignores the lower tail, the more familiar face of abnormality.", "d": "None of the three fails because the statistical criterion defines abnormality as deviation from the mean in both directions, which is option c."}}, {"q": "In an experiment the variable which is manipulated is known as:", "concept": "The answer is b: the variable the experimenter manipulates is the independent variable (IV). An experiment asks whether changing one thing causes a change in another. The IV is the factor the researcher deliberately varies, such as the dose of a drug, the teaching method or the amount of noise. The dependent variable (DV) is the behaviour measured to see whether the IV had an effect; its value 'depends' on the IV. All other factors that could affect the DV, the extraneous variables (time of day, room temperature, the ability of the participants), are held constant or otherwise controlled. Example: Riya tests whether noise affects concentration. One group solves maths sums in a quiet room and another in a room with loud traffic noise. Noise level is the IV, since she manipulates it; the number of sums solved correctly is the DV, since she measures it. A third kind is the intervening variable, an unseen process inside the organism that links the IV and the DV, such as fatigue, hunger or expectancy. Edward C. Tolman (1938) used the term for such inferred processes, and Kenneth MacCorquodale and Paul Meehl (1948) later separated intervening variables from hypothetical constructs. Option b is right because manipulation by the experimenter is the defining feature of the IV. Exam trap: the IV and DV are easily reversed; remember 'I change the IV, I measure the DV', and the DV depends on the IV.", "traps": {"a": "The dependent variable is the one measured, the outcome that depends on the IV (the sums solved in the noise study); the experimenter does not manipulate it.", "c": "An intervening variable is an unobserved process inside the organism, such as fatigue or hunger, inferred to link the IV and the DV (Edward C. Tolman's term); it is neither manipulated nor directly measured.", "d": "None of the three fails because the manipulated variable has a standard name, the independent variable (b)."}}, {"q": "Psychology is the science of:", "concept": "The answer is d: in its modern sense psychology is the science of behaviour, now usually stated as the scientific study of behaviour and mental processes. The word comes from the Greek psyche (soul) and logos (study), and its definition changed as the subject grew. Greek philosophers such as Aristotle (De Anima, about 350 BC) treated it as the study of the soul; but the soul cannot be observed or measured, so that definition was dropped. Later philosophers called it the science of the mind, which proved too vague. Wilhelm Wundt, who founded the first laboratory at Leipzig in 1879, made it the study of conscious experience, and William James (1890) called it 'the science of mental life'; but consciousness could only be studied by introspection, which is private and hard to check. John B. Watson (1913), in 'Psychology as the behaviorist views it', said psychology should study only observable behaviour, which others can measure and verify. Example: Meera's exam anxiety can be studied through what can be measured (her heart rate, her avoidance of revision, her anxiety test score), not through her 'soul'. Option d is right because behaviour is the observable, measurable subject matter that makes psychology a science, and it is the current definition among the four. Exam trap: the sequence soul, mind, consciousness, behaviour is a common question; soul is the oldest and behaviour the latest, so do not choose consciousness just because Wundt founded the science. Key note: the modern definition also includes mental processes, but of the four options behaviour is the current one, so d stands.", "traps": {"a": "Soul is the oldest definition, from the Greek psyche and philosophers such as Aristotle; it was dropped because the soul cannot be observed or measured.", "b": "Consciousness was the subject matter for Wilhelm Wundt and his student Edward Titchener (structuralism) and for William James (functionalism) in the late 1800s; behaviourism replaced it because introspection is private and unverifiable.", "c": "Mind was the philosophers' definition (17th and 18th centuries); it was abandoned as too vague and not open to direct observation."}}, {"q": "Social Psychology is the scientific study of the way individuals ______ in a social situation.", "concept": "The answer is d: social psychology studies how individuals think, feel and act in social situations. Gordon Allport (1954), in the Handbook of Social Psychology, gave the classic definition: social psychology is an attempt to understand and explain how the thought, feeling and behaviour of individuals are influenced by the actual, imagined or implied presence of other human beings. 'Actual' means others are really there (a crowd watching you), 'imagined' means we picture them (thinking what our parents would say), and 'implied' means social roles and norms act on us even when we are alone. All three sides of the person are covered. Thinking: social cognition, such as how we form impressions, explain others' behaviour (attribution) and hold stereotypes. Feeling: attitudes, prejudice, attraction, love and the emotions others arouse in us. Acting: conformity, obedience, helping, aggression and behaviour in groups. Example: Kabir sees a classmate cheat in an exam. What he thinks (is it my business?), what he feels (anger, fear of being called a tell-tale) and what he does (reports it or keeps quiet) are all shaped by the people around him, and social psychology studies all three. Option d is right because the field covers cognition, emotion and behaviour together, not one of them alone. Exam trap: some older definitions speak only of the behaviour of individuals in social situations, which tempts students to pick c; but Allport's definition and modern textbooks name thought and feeling as well.", "traps": {"a": "Thinking (social cognition: impressions, attributions, stereotypes) is part of social psychology, but only one of the three parts in the definition.", "b": "Feeling (attitudes, prejudice, attraction) is part of social psychology, but picking it alone leaves out thought and behaviour.", "c": "Acting (conformity, helping, aggression) is the behavioural part; older definitions stressed behaviour, but the standard definition also includes thought and feeling."}}, {"q": "A person faces a problem of Depression, what type of guidance he needs?", "concept": "The answer is c: a person facing depression needs personal guidance. Guidance is usually divided into three main types. Educational guidance helps with choosing courses and subjects, study habits, examinations and learning difficulties. Vocational guidance helps with choosing a career, preparing for it and entering it; Frank Parsons (1909) laid its foundation. Personal guidance (sometimes called personal-social guidance) helps with emotional, social, family and health problems and with adjustment to oneself and to others. Depression, a lasting low mood with loss of interest, poor sleep, low energy and feelings of worthlessness, is an emotional problem of the person, so it falls under personal guidance. The guidance worker listens, supports and helps the person understand his feelings and find ways of coping; if the depression is clinical (severe, long-lasting or with thoughts of suicide), he refers the person to a clinical psychologist or psychiatrist for treatment. Example: Arjun, a college student, has felt sad and hopeless for months, has stopped meeting friends and has lost interest in everything. His problem is not which subject or which job to choose but his emotional state, so he needs personal guidance and probably a referral. Option c is right because depression is an emotional and adjustment problem, the domain of personal guidance. Exam trap: 'All the three' tempts because depression often lowers marks and work output; but educational and vocational guidance deal with study and career choices, the side effects, not the emotional problem itself.", "traps": {"a": "Vocational guidance (founded by Frank Parsons) helps with choosing and preparing for a career; depression is not a career-choice problem.", "b": "Educational guidance helps with courses, study habits and learning difficulties; depression may lower marks, but the problem itself is emotional.", "d": "All the three overstates it: depression is an emotional problem handled by personal guidance (with referral if it is clinical), not by educational or vocational guidance."}}, {"q": "Types of Scales are:", "concept": "The answer is d: ordinal, nominal and interval are all types of measurement scale. Stanley Smith Stevens (1946), in his paper 'On the theory of scales of measurement' in Science, named four levels of measurement, each adding a property to the one below. Nominal: numbers or names only label categories, with no order (gender, blood group, roll numbers, house colours); you can count frequencies and find the mode. Ordinal: categories are ranked in order, but the gaps between ranks are not equal (class rank, socioeconomic class); the median and percentiles suit it. Interval: equal units between points, but the zero is arbitrary (temperature in Celsius; IQ scores are usually treated this way); the mean and SD can be used, but ratios such as 'twice as much' cannot. Ratio: equal units and a true zero (height, weight, reaction time, income); all statistics, including ratios, are meaningful. A memory aid is NOIR: nominal, ordinal, interval, ratio. Example: in a school race, Meera's bib number 27 is nominal, her finishing position of 2nd is ordinal, the temperature that day (30 degrees Celsius) is interval, and her time of 52 seconds is ratio. Option d is right because each of the three listed is one of Stevens' four scales; the fourth, ratio, is simply not offered. Exam trap: students sometimes think only the 'higher' scales count as real measurement; but nominal and ordinal are scales too, and the property that separates the top two is the zero (arbitrary in interval, true in ratio).", "traps": {"a": "Ordinal (rank order, such as class rank) is a scale type, but choosing it alone ignores that nominal and interval are also scales.", "b": "Nominal (labels and categories, such as roll numbers) is a scale type, but it is only one of the three listed.", "c": "Interval (equal units with an arbitrary zero, such as Celsius temperature) is a scale type, but nominal and ordinal are scales too."}}, {"q": "A belief that one is being spied on, threatened or otherwise mistreated is a delusion of:", "concept": "The answer is c: the belief that one is being spied on, threatened or otherwise mistreated is a delusion of persecution. A delusion is a fixed, false belief, held firmly despite clear evidence against it and not explained by the person's culture or religion. It is a disorder of the content of thought, unlike a hallucination, which is a disorder of perception. In a persecutory delusion the person believes he is being followed, watched, plotted against, poisoned, cheated or harassed, often by neighbours, family, the police or a secret group. It is the most common type of delusion and is seen in schizophrenia, in delusional disorder (persecutory type) and in severe mood disorders with psychotic features. Example: Kabir is sure his neighbours have hidden cameras in his walls and are mixing poison into his food; no evidence or reassurance shakes him, and he stops eating at home. That is a persecutory delusion. Other common types are grandeur (special powers, wealth or identity), control (an outside force controls one's thoughts or actions), reference (ordinary events carry special messages about oneself), jealousy (a partner's unfaithfulness) and nihilistic (oneself or the world does not exist). Option c is right because being spied on, threatened and mistreated is the defining content of persecution. Exam trap: delusions of reference often travel with persecution (the news is about me, people on the bus are talking about me), but the specific belief that one is being harmed or plotted against is persecutory.", "traps": {"a": "A delusion of grandeur (grandiose delusion) is the belief that one has special powers, wealth, fame or an exalted identity, common in mania; it is not about being harmed.", "b": "A delusion of control (passivity) is the belief that an outside force controls one's thoughts, feelings or actions, one of Kurt Schneider's first-rank symptoms of schizophrenia; it is not about being spied on or mistreated.", "d": "None of the three fails because the belief described is the textbook persecutory delusion (c)."}}, {"q": "The first Psychological Laboratory was established in:", "concept": "The answer is d: the first psychological laboratory was established at Leipzig, Germany. Wilhelm Wundt set it up at the University of Leipzig in 1879, and that date is taken as the birth of psychology as a separate experimental science, independent of philosophy and physiology. In the laboratory Wundt and his students measured reaction times, studied sensation, perception and attention, and trained observers in introspection, careful reporting of conscious experience under controlled conditions. His approach, carried to America by his student Edward Titchener (at Cornell from 1892), became structuralism. Many early psychologists trained there, including James McKeen Cattell, who took his doctorate under Wundt in 1886, and Leipzig became the model for laboratories around the world. Example: a student like Riya in Wundt's laboratory would press a key the moment she heard a click, and her reaction time would be measured in thousandths of a second and compared across conditions. Option d is right because Leipzig, with Wundt's 1879 laboratory, is the standard answer for the first formal psychology laboratory. Exam trap: William James had a small demonstration room at Harvard (in Cambridge, next to Boston) by about 1875, so Boston is tempting; but it was used for teaching demonstrations, not systematic research, so Wundt's Leipzig laboratory is counted first. G. Stanley Hall opened the first American research laboratory at Johns Hopkins University, Baltimore, in 1883.", "traps": {"a": "Boston points to William James's small demonstration laboratory at Harvard (Cambridge, about 1875), used for teaching rather than research; it is not counted as the first psychology laboratory.", "b": "California points to Lewis Terman, who published the Stanford-Binet intelligence test at Stanford in 1916; no laboratory there was the first.", "c": "Berlin points to Hermann Ebbinghaus's memory studies (1885) and Carl Stumpf's institute, where the Gestalt psychologists Wolfgang Kohler and Kurt Koffka trained; both came after Leipzig."}}, {"q": "Case study is related to ______ Psychology.", "concept": "The answer is a: the case study is the method most closely tied to clinical psychology. A case study is an intensive, in-depth study of one individual (sometimes one family or group), built up from many sources: a detailed case history (family background, childhood, education, health, relationships), interviews with the person and those close to him, observation, psychological tests, and records such as school reports or medical files. Clinical psychology assesses and treats one person's problems at a time, so the method suits it exactly: the clinician must understand this person's history to diagnose the problem and plan treatment. Lightner Witmer opened the first psychological clinic at the University of Pennsylvania in 1896 and coined the term 'clinical psychology'. Sigmund Freud built psychoanalysis on case studies such as Anna O. (with Josef Breuer, 1895) and Little Hans (1909). Example: Meera, aged 9, refuses to go to school. The psychologist takes a case history from her parents, talks to her teacher, observes her and gives tests, then writes up the whole picture with a treatment plan. Option a is right because the case study is the core method of clinical work, which studies one person in depth for diagnosis and treatment. Exam trap: case studies also appear in neuropsychology (Phineas Gage, the patient H.M.) and in research, but their weakness, that one case cannot be generalised, keeps them from being the main method of experimental psychology, which relies on controlled experiments with groups.", "traps": {"b": "Experimental psychology studies behaviour through controlled experiments on groups, manipulating an independent variable; it seeks general laws, not an in-depth picture of one person.", "c": "General psychology covers the basic principles of behaviour common to all normal people (learning, memory, perception); it relies on experiment and observation, not mainly on the case study.", "d": "None of the three fails because the case study is the hallmark method of clinical psychology (a)."}}, {"q": "To understand the child rearing practices of tribal people, the best method is:", "concept": "The answer is a: participant observation is the best method for studying the child-rearing practices of tribal people. In participant observation the researcher lives in the community for a long time, takes part in its daily life, and watches behaviour from the inside as it happens naturally. Over months the people get used to him and act normally. It is the classic method of anthropology and ethnography. Bronislaw Malinowski (1922) lived among the Trobriand Islanders to write Argonauts of the Western Pacific, and Margaret Mead (1928) lived in Samoa for Coming of Age in Samoa, a study of adolescence and upbringing. Child rearing suits this method well: feeding, weaning, toilet training, discipline and play happen every day at home, are partly unspoken habits, and parents may not describe them accurately. Tribal people may also be unused to forms and wary of outsiders asking questions. Example: Kabir, a research student, spends six months in a Bhil village, helps with daily chores and records how mothers carry, feed and discipline their children. Option a is right because only participant observation records what tribal parents actually do, in their natural setting, from the inside. Exam trap: surveys and interviews record what people say they do, not what they actually do, and they need a shared language and trust. Key note: anthropologists combine observation with interviews, so d is tempting; but the stem asks for the best single method, and participant observation is the textbook answer for studying a culture from inside, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "A field survey collects data from many people with questionnaires or schedules; it is quick and broad, but it records only what people report and misses private, everyday practices.", "c": "An interview gathers information by direct questioning; it depends on what parents choose to say and on trust and a shared language, so it supports observation rather than replacing it.", "d": "All the three overstates it: surveys and interviews can add to the picture, but the stem asks for the best method, which is participant observation."}}, {"q": "Establishment of Central Bureau of Educational and Vocational Guidance in 1954 was the result of the recommendations of:", "concept": "The answer is a: the Central Bureau of Educational and Vocational Guidance (1954) was set up as a result of the recommendations of the Secondary Education Commission. After independence, India appointed commissions to review its education system. The Secondary Education Commission (1952 to 1953), chaired by Dr A. Lakshmanaswami Mudaliar and so also called the Mudaliar Commission, studied secondary schooling. It recommended that educational and vocational guidance become part of secondary education, with trained guidance officers and career masters made available to schools. Acting on this, the Ministry of Education set up the Central Bureau of Educational and Vocational Guidance in Delhi in 1954 to train guidance workers and prepare tests and career information; state guidance bureaus followed, and the Central Bureau was later absorbed into the National Council of Educational Research and Training (NCERT). Example: when a school counsellor gives Riya, in Class 9, an aptitude test and talks her through the choice of stream, she is doing the work the Mudaliar Commission wanted in every secondary school. Option a is right because the Mudaliar Commission reported in 1953, just before the Bureau was founded, and its report made guidance a formal recommendation for secondary schools. Exam trap: the Kothari Commission is the best-known Indian education commission and also stressed guidance, so students pick b; but it sat from 1964 to 1966, a decade after the Bureau was founded, so it cannot be the cause.", "traps": {"b": "The Kothari Commission (Education Commission, 1964 to 1966, chaired by Dr D. S. Kothari) came a decade after the Bureau was founded; it later recommended guidance from the primary stage.", "c": "The University Education Commission (1948 to 1949, chaired by Dr S. Radhakrishnan) dealt with higher education and universities, not school guidance services.", "d": "None of the three fails because the Secondary Education Commission (the Mudaliar Commission, a) made the recommendation."}}, {"q": "A student's rank in a class involves the scale:", "concept": "The answer is b: a student's rank in class is measured on an ordinal scale. An ordinal scale puts people or objects in order of more or less of something, but it does not tell how far apart they are. Rank 1 is above rank 2 and rank 2 above rank 3, yet the gaps need not be equal. Example: in a class test, Riya scores 95 and comes first, Arjun scores 94 and comes second, and Meera scores 74 and comes third. The ranks 1, 2, 3 look evenly spaced, but the real gap between first and second is 1 mark and between second and third is 20 marks. So ranks show position, not amount. This scheme comes from Stanley Smith Stevens (1946), who named four scales: nominal (labels only), ordinal (order), interval (equal units, arbitrary zero) and ratio (equal units, true zero). Statistics suited to ordinal data are the median, percentiles and rank-order correlation such as Charles Spearman's (1904) rho, rather than the mean. Option b is right because ranking gives order without equal intervals, which is the definition of ordinal measurement. Exam trap: ranks are written as numbers, so students think they are interval; but numbers do not make a scale interval unless the units between them are equal. The marks themselves are a higher level of measurement; the ranks made from them are ordinal.", "traps": {"a": "A nominal scale only labels categories with no order (roll numbers, house colours); a rank shows order, which nominal data lack.", "c": "An interval scale has equal units with an arbitrary zero (Celsius temperature); ranks do not have equal gaps, so they are not interval.", "d": "A ratio scale has equal units and a true zero (height, weight, reaction time); rank has neither equal units nor a meaningful zero."}}, {"q": "During hallucinations a person:", "concept": "The answer is c: during hallucinations a person may both see and hear things that are not really there. A hallucination is a perception that occurs without any external stimulus, yet feels as real and vivid as a normal perception and seems to come from outside the person. It can occur in any sense. Auditory hallucinations (hearing voices that comment on the person or give commands) are the most common type in schizophrenia. Visual hallucinations (seeing people, animals or lights) are common in delirium, drug states and some dementias. There are also tactile ones (feeling insects crawling under the skin, called formication, as in cocaine use or alcohol withdrawal), olfactory ones (smells) and gustatory ones (tastes). Example: Arjun, in severe alcohol withdrawal, sees small animals running across the wall and hears his late father calling his name, though nothing is there; both experiences are hallucinations. Option c is right because hallucinations are not limited to one sense, so seeing and hearing things that are not present are both hallucinations. Exam trap: a hallucination is easily confused with an illusion and with a delusion. An illusion is a misperception of something real (a rope at dusk seen as a snake), and a delusion is a false belief, not a perception. If there is no stimulus at all, it is a hallucination.", "traps": {"a": "Seeing things that are not there is a visual hallucination, but it is only half the answer, since hearing absent things is also a hallucination.", "b": "Hearing things that are not there is an auditory hallucination, the commonest type in schizophrenia, but hallucinations occur in vision and the other senses too.", "d": "None of the three fails because both seeing and hearing absent things are hallucinations (c)."}}, {"q": "Controls are must in design:", "concept": "The answer is d: controls are a must in all three designs, the one group, the parallel group and the rotation group design. Control is the essence of an experiment. To show that the independent variable (IV) caused a change in the dependent variable (DV), the researcher must compare the experimental condition with a control condition and hold all other (extraneous) variables constant. There are three classic designs. In the one group (single group) design, the same group is tested first under the control condition and then under the experimental condition, so it serves as its own control. In the parallel group (equivalent group) design, two groups are made equal by matching or random assignment: the experimental group receives the IV and the control group does not. In the rotation group design, two groups swap conditions in turn, so each serves once as the experimental and once as the control group, which cancels out differences between the groups. Example: Meera tests whether music helps memory. In the rotation design, group A learns word lists with music first and group B in silence first, then they switch. Option d is right because each design builds in a control condition and needs extraneous variables controlled. Exam trap: the one group design looks as if it has no control because there is no second group; in fact its own control-condition scores are the comparison. Key note: if 'controls' is read as a separate control group, only the parallel group design has one, so b is tempting; but each of the three designs builds in its own control condition and control of extraneous variables is a must in all of them, so d stands.", "traps": {"a": "The one group design uses the same group as its own control (control condition first, experimental condition second); it needs controls, but so do the other two designs.", "b": "The parallel group design has a separate control group matched to the experimental group; it needs controls, but the one group and rotation designs also build in control conditions.", "c": "The rotation group design swaps the groups between experimental and control conditions to cancel group differences; it needs controls, but it is not the only design that does."}}, {"q": "Sociability as a trait is mostly determined by:", "concept": "The answer is c: sociability as a trait is determined by both heredity and environment. Sociability is the tendency to seek out and enjoy the company of others, a central part of extraversion. Arnold Buss and Robert Plomin (1984) listed it, with emotionality and activity, as one of three temperaments (the EAS model) that appear early in life and are partly inherited. Twin studies show the genetic part: identical twins, who share all their genes, are more alike in sociability and extraversion than fraternal twins, who share about half. The Minnesota Study of Twins Reared Apart, reported by Thomas Bouchard and colleagues (1990), found identical twins raised in different homes about as alike in personality as those raised together, with heritability of about 0.40 to 0.50. So roughly half the variation between people is genetic, and the rest comes from the environment: family, friends, school, culture and chance experiences. Jerome Kagan (1988) found that very shy, inhibited toddlers often stayed cautious, yet many became less shy as they grew. Heredity sets a range; environment decides where in that range a child ends up. Example: Riya was a shy baby like her mother, but her parents took her to play groups and a drama club, and she grew into a fairly sociable teenager. Option c is right because sociability is about half heritable and half shaped by experience. Exam trap: the word 'mostly' pushes students to pick one side; but a heritability near 0.50 means neither heredity nor environment dominates, so both is the standard answer.", "traps": {"a": "Heredity matters (twin studies give heritability of about 0.40 to 0.50, and Buss and Plomin's EAS model treats sociability as an inherited temperament), but it explains only about half the variation.", "b": "Environment (family, peers, culture) matters, but twin and adoption studies show a strong genetic part too, so environment alone is not the answer.", "d": "None of the three fails because sociability is clearly shaped by both heredity and environment (c)."}}, {"q": "Socialisation can be best facilitated by:", "concept": "The answer is b: socialisation is best facilitated by outdoor games. Socialisation is the process by which a child learns the values, norms, roles and social skills of his group and becomes a functioning member of society. The family starts it; peers, school and play carry it further. Mildred Parten (1932) showed that play itself becomes more social with age, moving from solitary and parallel play (alone, or beside others) to associative play (playing together and sharing materials) and finally cooperative play, in which children share a goal and take roles. Outdoor team games such as kabaddi, kho-kho, cricket and football are cooperative play at its fullest. They bring many children together every day, and each game demands social skills: cooperating with teammates, taking turns, following rules, accepting the umpire's decision, leading and following, controlling anger and accepting defeat. Example: Kabir, a shy boy, joins the school kho-kho team. Within months he has learned to plan moves with teammates, wait his turn and shake hands after losing, skills he never picked up from video games at home. Option b is right because outdoor team games give the most regular, rule-bound group interaction, which is what socialisation needs. Exam trap: excursions sound social because the whole class goes together, but they are occasional, so their effect is small compared with daily play. Key note: excursions and indoor games also help socialisation; outdoor team games are the textbook answer because they give the most regular group interaction, so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Indoor games (chess, carrom, video games) are mostly solitary or played by two, so they give far less group interaction than outdoor team games.", "c": "Excursions do bring a class together and teach cooperation, but they are occasional, while outdoor games provide group interaction every day.", "d": "None of the three fails because outdoor games (b) are the standard textbook answer for socialisation."}}, {"q": "Frank Parsons is associated with ______ guidance.", "concept": "The answer is b: Frank Parsons is associated with vocational guidance; he is called the father of vocational guidance. Frank Parsons (1854 to 1908), a Boston engineer, lawyer and social reformer, opened the Vocation Bureau in Boston in 1908 to help young people, many of them poor or immigrants, choose suitable work instead of drifting into whatever job came first. His book Choosing a Vocation appeared in 1909, after his death. In it he set out the first model of career choice, later called the trait-and-factor approach, in three steps: (1) a clear understanding of yourself, your aptitudes, abilities, interests and limitations; (2) knowledge of the requirements, conditions, pay and prospects of different kinds of work; and (3) 'true reasoning' on the relation between these two sets of facts, that is, matching the person to the job. This matching idea still underlies career counselling and John Holland's (1959) theory of matching personality types to work environments, later known as the RIASEC types. Example: Meera, good at drawing and interested in buildings, learns what architecture demands and offers, and decides it fits her; that is Parsons' three-step method in action. Option b is right because Parsons' work was about choosing an occupation, which is vocational guidance. Exam trap: educational guidance is a different strand credited to others: Jesse B. Davis brought guidance into the Grand Rapids schools in 1907, and Truman Kelley (1914) first used the term 'educational guidance'.", "traps": {"a": "Educational guidance (choice of courses, study problems) is linked to Jesse B. Davis in Grand Rapids (1907) and to Truman Kelley, who first used the term in 1914, not to Parsons.", "c": "Personal guidance (emotional, social and adjustment problems) grew later with the mental hygiene and counselling movements; Parsons' work was on choosing a job.", "d": "All the three overstates it: Parsons founded vocational guidance specifically, through his Vocation Bureau (1908) and Choosing a Vocation (1909)."}}, {"q": "Ratio scales have:", "concept": "The answer is a: ratio scales have a true (absolute) zero. On a ratio scale zero means a complete absence of the quantity being measured, and the units are equal all along the scale. Because zero is real, ratios make sense: 10 kg is twice 5 kg, and a reaction time of 400 milliseconds is twice one of 200 milliseconds. Examples are height, weight, age, income, reaction time and the number of errors made. The interval scale also has equal units, but its zero is arbitrary, a point fixed by convention: 0 degrees Celsius is the freezing point of water, not 'no temperature', so 40 degrees is not twice as hot as 20 degrees. IQ is treated the same way: an IQ of 0 would not mean no intelligence, and an IQ of 140 is not twice an IQ of 70. These levels were set out by Stanley Smith Stevens (1946), who ordered the four scales nominal, ordinal, interval and ratio, with ratio the highest because it has every property. Example: Arjun takes 30 seconds to solve a puzzle and Riya takes 60; saying Riya took twice as long is meaningful because time has a true zero. Option a is right because a true zero is the property that sets the ratio scale apart from the interval scale. Exam trap: students mix up the two scales that have equal units; remember that interval has an arbitrary zero and ratio a true one, and no scale can have both kinds of zero.", "traps": {"b": "An arbitrary zero belongs to the interval scale (Celsius temperature, IQ), where zero does not mean absence of the quantity.", "c": "Both is impossible: a scale's zero is either a real absence (ratio) or a convention (interval), not both at once.", "d": "None of the three fails because the defining feature of a ratio scale is a true zero (a)."}}, {"q": "A patient washes hands repeatedly in:", "concept": "The answer is c: repeated hand washing is the classic compulsion of obsessive-compulsive neurosis, now called obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD). OCD has two parts. Obsessions are intrusive, unwanted thoughts, images or urges that cause anxiety, such as the idea that one's hands are dirty or contaminated. Compulsions are repetitive acts or mental rituals done to reduce that anxiety, such as washing, checking, counting or ordering. The relief is brief, so the ritual returns and grows. Contamination fear with washing is the commonest picture. Example: Kabir washes his hands 40 times a day after touching door handles; he knows the fear is excessive, yet feels unbearable tension if he stops. The old name, obsessive-compulsive neurosis, is the term used in the second Diagnostic and Statistical Manual (DSM-II, 1968), which listed it among the neuroses. DSM-III (1980) to DSM-IV-TR placed OCD among the anxiety disorders, and DSM-5 (2013) gave it its own chapter, Obsessive-Compulsive and Related Disorders, with body dysmorphic disorder, hoarding, trichotillomania and excoriation. Treatment is exposure and response prevention (touching the 'dirty' object without washing) and SSRI medicines. Option c is right because the stem describes a compulsion, the defining sign of OCD. Exam trap: hysteria is the old name for conversion and dissociative disorders, not for rituals, and 'anxiety' is the broad family, not the specific diagnosis. Key note: OCD was classed as an anxiety disorder from DSM-III to DSM-IV-TR, so a is tempting; but repeated hand washing is the hallmark compulsion of OCD itself, so the answer used here, c, stands.", "traps": {"a": "Anxiety is the broad family: OCD sat inside the anxiety disorders from DSM-III to DSM-IV-TR, but 'anxiety' alone names no specific disorder, and hand-washing rituals point to OCD in particular.", "b": "Hysteria is the old term for conversion and dissociative disorders (loss of a function or of memory without a physical cause), not for repetitive washing rituals.", "d": "All the three fails because hysteria has nothing to do with compulsive washing and anxiety is only the umbrella; the specific answer is obsessive-compulsive neurosis."}}, {"q": "If one parent provides dominant gene for brown hair colour and the other gives recessive gene for the brown hair, the offspring will have:", "concept": "The answer is a: the child will have brown hair, because a dominant allele shows itself even when it is paired with a recessive one. Gregor Mendel (1866), crossing pea plants, found that each trait is set by a pair of inherited factors, now called genes, one from each parent. The alternative forms of a gene are alleles. Two different alleles make a person heterozygous. In dominant-recessive inheritance the heterozygote shows only the dominant allele's trait (the phenotype); the recessive allele is masked but carried silently, so the child is a carrier who may pass it to a later generation. Example: Meera's father gives her the dominant brown allele (B) and her mother a recessive allele (b). Meera is Bb, so her hair is brown; if she later has children with another carrier, one child in four (bb) could show the recessive trait. Option a is right because a Bb child expresses the dominant brown allele. Exam trap: 'both' would need codominance (blood group AB shows both A and B) or incomplete dominance (red and white snapdragons giving pink); when one allele is stated to be dominant there is no mixing, and only the dominant trait shows. Key note: the stem is loosely worded ('recessive gene for the brown hair' means the recessive allele of the hair-colour gene), and real hair colour is polygenic, but on the one-gene textbook model the dominant brown allele shows, so the answer used here, a, stands.", "traps": {"b": "Black hair is not in this cross at all; neither parent's allele codes for black, so the child cannot show it.", "c": "Two colours at once would need codominance (as in blood group AB) or blending; under Mendel's law of dominance only one hair colour, the dominant one, shows.", "d": "None of the three is wrong because brown hair, the dominant trait, is listed as option a."}}, {"q": "Identity crisis is related to:", "concept": "The answer is c: identity crisis belongs to adolescence, the fifth of Erik Erikson's eight psychosocial stages. Erik Erikson (1950, Childhood and Society; 1968, Identity: Youth and Crisis) described life as eight stages, each with a 'crisis', a turning point between two outcomes, set by the meeting of the growing person with society's demands. In adolescence (about 12 to 18 years) the crisis is identity versus role confusion: the young person asks 'Who am I? What do I believe? What will I become?' and tries out roles, friendships, values and career ideas. Success gives fidelity, a stable sense of self; failure leaves role confusion. Erikson himself coined the term 'identity crisis'. Example: Arjun, 16, swings between wanting to be a cricketer, an engineer and a musician, and argues with his parents about religion; this trying out is normal identity work, not illness. James Marcia (1966) later sorted adolescents into four identity statuses: diffusion, foreclosure, moratorium and achievement. Option c is right because Erikson placed the identity crisis in adolescence. Exam trap: every stage has its own crisis, and examiners swap them: infancy is trust versus mistrust, early childhood autonomy versus shame and doubt, the play age initiative versus guilt, the school age industry versus inferiority, young adulthood intimacy versus isolation, adulthood generativity versus stagnation, and old age integrity versus despair.", "traps": {"a": "Infancy (birth to about 1 year) is Erikson's stage of trust versus mistrust, built on reliable care, not the question 'who am I?'.", "b": "Childhood holds autonomy versus shame and doubt, initiative versus guilt and industry versus inferiority; the identity crisis comes only when puberty and choices about the future arrive.", "d": "Adulthood holds intimacy versus isolation and generativity versus stagnation; adults can question themselves, but Erikson tied the identity crisis to adolescence."}}, {"q": "A student is least liked by the classmates in the class, he is:", "concept": "The answer is c: in sociometry, a student whom classmates do not choose is an isolate, the low-acceptance position in the group. Jacob L. Moreno (1934, Who Shall Survive?) devised sociometry to map attraction and rejection inside a group. Each member names, privately, the classmates he would most like to sit, play or work with (and sometimes those he would not). The choices are drawn as a sociogram, with circles for members and arrows for choices. A star receives many choices and is the most popular; an isolate receives none; a neglectee receives only a few; a rejectee is actively rejected by many; a mutual pair choose each other, and a clique chooses only within itself. Teachers use the sociogram to find lonely children and to plan seating and group work. Example: in Class 8, Riya is named by 15 classmates (a star), while Sameer is named by no one (an isolate); the class teacher pairs Sameer with a friendly classmate for projects. Option c is right because, of the options, isolate is the only sociometric label for a child at the bottom of peer acceptance. Exam trap: popular and star both describe the most liked child, the exact opposite of the stem. Key note: strictly, a child actively disliked is a 'rejectee' and an isolate is one nobody chooses; rejectee is not offered, and of the options isolate is the low-acceptance position, so the answer used here, c, stands.", "traps": {"a": "Popular describes a child chosen by many classmates, the opposite of the least liked child.", "b": "Star is Jacob Moreno's sociometric term for the member who receives the most choices, the most liked child, the reverse of the stem.", "d": "None of the three is wrong because isolate, the sociometric label for a child nobody chooses, is listed as option c."}}, {"q": "In guidance, appraisal of client is needed:", "concept": "The answer is c: appraisal of the client is needed always in guidance, because no one can be guided without first being understood. Appraisal (also called the individual inventory or student appraisal service) is the collection and study of information about the person: intelligence, aptitude, achievement and interest tests, the cumulative record, interviews, observation, rating scales, autobiography and the case study. It is the base on which the other guidance services stand: orientation, information, counselling, placement and follow-up. Frank Parsons (1909, Choosing a Vocation) made it the first of his three steps: a clear understanding of yourself, your aptitudes, abilities and interests; then knowledge of the world of work; then true reasoning to match the two. Appraisal is also continuous, not a one-time event, because a pupil's abilities, interests and problems change as he grows. Example: before advising Neha on choosing science or commerce after Class 10, the counsellor studies her marks over three years, an aptitude test, an interest inventory and a talk with her parents; without these, the advice would be guesswork. Option c is right because every guidance decision rests on knowing the individual, so appraisal is needed in every case and all through the process. Exam trap: 'sometimes' sounds moderate and wise, but guidance texts treat appraisal as the foundation of all guidance, not an optional extra used only for difficult cases.", "traps": {"a": "Hardly would turn guidance into blind advice; guidance texts treat knowing the individual as the starting point of every service.", "b": "Not at all contradicts the whole idea of guidance, which is help fitted to one person's abilities, interests and problems, and that needs appraisal.", "d": "Sometimes treats appraisal as optional, but it is the base of orientation, information, counselling and placement, and it must go on continuously."}}, {"q": "Which is most important in test construction?", "concept": "The answer is b: validity is the most important quality in test construction. Validity is the degree to which a test measures what it claims to measure. The Standards for Educational and Psychological Testing, issued jointly by the American Educational Research Association, the American Psychological Association and the National Council on Measurement in Education (1999 and 2014 editions), call validity the most fundamental consideration in developing and evaluating tests. Reliability is consistency: the test gives about the same result on repeated use. Objectivity is agreement between scorers, free of the examiner's personal judgement. Both are needed, but they serve validity. Reliability is necessary but not sufficient: a test can be reliable without being valid, yet it cannot be valid without being reliable, because the validity coefficient cannot exceed the square root of the reliability coefficient. Example: Arjun's teacher builds a 'memory test' whose scores in fact depend mostly on reading speed; pupils get almost the same scores each time (reliable) and every scorer agrees (objective), yet it tells her little about memory (not valid). In the same way, head size can be measured very consistently, but it is worthless as a test of intelligence. Option b is right because a test that does not measure what it claims is worthless, however consistent or objective it is. Exam trap: 'All the three' looks safe because a good test needs reliability, objectivity, validity, norms and usability; but the stem asks for the most important one. Key note: a good test needs all three, so d is tempting, and some Indian texts list objectivity first; but the stem asks which is most important, and most textbooks and the testing Standards name validity, so the answer used here, b, stands.", "traps": {"a": "Reliability is consistency of scores (test-retest, split-half); it is necessary for validity but not sufficient, since a test can be consistently wrong.", "c": "Objectivity is agreement between scorers, free from personal bias; it supports reliability, but an objectively scored test can still measure the wrong thing.", "d": "All three are qualities of a good test, but the stem asks for the most important one, and the testing Standards give that place to validity."}}, {"q": "Fear of darkness is known as:", "concept": "The answer is d: fear of darkness (or of the night) is nyctophobia, from the Greek nyx, night, and phobos, fear. A phobia is an intense, irrational fear of a specific object or situation, out of proportion to the real danger, that leads to avoidance. In DSM-5-TR this is a specific phobia: marked fear or anxiety almost always set off by the object or situation, which is avoided or endured with intense distress, usually lasting 6 months or more and causing distress or impairment. Fear of the dark is common and normal in young children; it becomes a phobia when it persists and disrupts life. Example: Kabir, 22, cannot sleep without a light on and refuses to travel at night; even the thought of a power cut makes his heart race. Phobia names are built from Greek roots: acro (peak, heights), agora (marketplace), zoo (animal), claustro (closed space), hydro (water), nycto (night). Treatment is graded exposure, as in systematic desensitisation, developed by Joseph Wolpe (1958). Option d is right because nycto means night, so nyctophobia is fear of the dark. Exam trap: agoraphobia is often misread as fear of the dark or of strangers; it is fear of places where escape is hard, such as crowds, open markets and public transport, and DSM-5 lists it as a separate anxiety disorder, not a specific phobia.", "traps": {"a": "Acrophobia (Greek akron, peak) is fear of heights, such as balconies, ladders and bridges, not of darkness.", "b": "Agoraphobia (Greek agora, marketplace) is fear of places or situations where escape may be hard, such as crowds, open spaces or public transport; DSM-5 lists it as a separate disorder.", "c": "Zoophobia is fear of animals in general; specific forms include cynophobia (dogs) and ophidiophobia (snakes)."}}, {"q": "Intelligence is related to a gland:", "concept": "The answer is a: the thyroid is the gland most directly tied to intelligence, because its hormone, thyroxine, is essential for brain development. The thyroid, in the neck, releases thyroxine (T4) and triiodothyronine (T3), which set the body's metabolic rate and, in the foetus and infant, support the growth of neurons, myelin and synapses. Too little thyroid hormone from birth (congenital hypothyroidism, once called cretinism) causes intellectual disability and stunted growth unless it is treated in the first weeks of life, which is why newborns are screened for it. The thyroid needs iodine to make thyroxine, and the World Health Organization calls iodine deficiency the world's most common preventable cause of brain damage; India iodises salt for this reason. In adults, hypothyroidism slows thinking and causes lethargy (myxoedema), while hyperthyroidism (Graves' disease) causes anxiety and restlessness. Example: baby Aarav's newborn screening shows low thyroxine; daily thyroxine tablets started at once let him develop normally. Option a is right because a shortage of the thyroid's own hormone in early life directly impairs intellectual development. Exam trap: the pituitary is the 'master gland', so it is often picked; but its own growth hormone governs height (gigantism, dwarfism), not intelligence. Key note: the pituitary controls the thyroid through thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH), so b is tempting; but the gland whose own hormone deficit directly causes intellectual disability is the thyroid, so the answer used here, a, stands.", "traps": {"b": "The pituitary is the 'master gland' that releases growth hormone and controls other glands (including the thyroid, through TSH); its own hormones govern growth, not intelligence.", "c": "The adrenals release cortisol and adrenaline for the stress (fight or flight) response, not for brain development or intelligence.", "d": "The gonads (testes and ovaries) release sex hormones such as testosterone and oestrogen, which drive puberty and sexual characteristics, not intelligence."}}, {"q": "Chimpanzee is to Kohler as cat is to:", "concept": "The answer is a: chimpanzees go with Wolfgang Kohler and cats with Edward Thorndike, the classic animals of insight learning and trial-and-error learning. Edward Thorndike (1898, Animal Intelligence) shut hungry cats in puzzle boxes; a cat could reach food outside only by pulling a loop or pressing a lever. At first it scratched and clawed at random, and by accident it hit the latch. Over trials the useless moves dropped out and escape time fell gradually: trial-and-error learning. He summed this up in the law of effect: responses followed by satisfaction are 'stamped in', those followed by discomfort 'stamped out'. Wolfgang Kohler (1917 in German; The Mentality of Apes, 1925 in English), working on Tenerife from 1913 to 1920, placed bananas out of reach of chimpanzees; Sultan joined two sticks to rake them in, and others stacked boxes, suddenly, after a pause. This was insight learning, a sudden grasp of the relations in a problem. Example: Ananya learns a new phone by pressing buttons until one works (Thorndike's trial and error), but solves a riddle in a flash of understanding (Kohler's insight). Option a is right because Thorndike's cats in puzzle boxes are as tied to him as chimpanzees are to Kohler. Exam trap: examiners shuffle the animal pairings; Ivan Pavlov's animal was the dog, B. F. Skinner's the rat and the pigeon.", "traps": {"b": "B. F. Skinner (1938) studied rats pressing levers and pigeons pecking keys in the Skinner box (operant conditioning), not cats in puzzle boxes.", "c": "Ivan Pavlov (1927) studied dogs salivating to a signal paired with food (classical conditioning).", "d": "Clark Hull (1943) built his drive-reduction theory mainly on experiments with rats, not cats in puzzle boxes."}}, {"q": "Barrier/s to group cohesiveness is/are:", "concept": "The answer is c: both different languages and different castes are barriers to group cohesiveness. Group cohesiveness is the strength of the bonds that hold members in a group: how much they like the group, each other and its goals. Leon Festinger, Stanley Schachter and Kurt Back (1950) defined it as 'the total field of forces which act on members to remain in the group'. Cohesion rises with similarity of attitudes and background, shared goals, frequent interaction, small size, an outside threat and success; it falls with anything that divides members. Different languages block communication: members cannot share ideas, jokes or plans easily, and they drift into language subgroups. Different castes bring status divisions and prejudice, so members sort themselves into in-groups and out-groups and trust each other less. Same religion, by contrast, is a shared similarity, which tends to bind members together. Example: in a hostel cricket team, Tamil-speaking and Hindi-speaking players who cannot talk freely, and players who keep to their own caste circles, pass and plan less well than a team that shares a language and mixes freely. Option c is right because each of a and b splits the group into subgroups and weakens the forces holding it together. Exam trap: 'same religion' is planted as a distractor; similarity raises cohesion, so it supports a group rather than blocking it.", "traps": {"a": "Different languages are a real barrier because they block communication, but picking a alone misses caste divisions, which also split the group.", "b": "Different castes are a real barrier because status divisions and prejudice create in-groups and out-groups, but picking b alone misses the language barrier.", "d": "Same religion is a shared similarity, which tends to raise cohesion, so it is a support rather than a barrier."}}, {"q": "In order to deal with educational problems counsellor needs:", "concept": "The answer is b: to deal with a pupil's educational problems, the counsellor needs the cumulative record card. The cumulative record card is a continuous, year-by-year record of a pupil's development kept by the school: marks in each subject, attendance, intelligence, aptitude and achievement test scores, health, family background, co-curricular activities, personality traits and teachers' remarks. Because it builds up over the years and moves with the pupil from class to class, it shows trends that a single meeting cannot: a fall in marks after a change of school, a long illness, a gap between ability and achievement. That makes it the main tool of educational guidance: diagnosing backwardness and underachievement, choosing subjects and streams, and spotting learning difficulties. Example: Rohan's maths marks fell from 80 in Class 6 to 45 in Class 8; his card shows average intelligence and many absences through illness in Class 7, so the counsellor arranges remedial maths instead of labelling him 'weak'. Option b is right because the card holds the pupil's whole school history in one place, which is exactly what educational problems need. Exam trap: the case study is an in-depth study of one problem case that uses the cumulative record as one of its sources, and the interview is a face-to-face talk; both help, but neither is the standing school record. Key note: case study and interview also help a counsellor; but for educational problems the cumulative record card is the core tool, as it holds the pupil's whole school history in one place, so the answer used here, b, stands.", "traps": {"a": "A case study is an in-depth study of one individual's problem drawn from many sources (including the cumulative record); it is kept for serious problem cases, not used as the standard tool for educational problems.", "c": "The interview is a face-to-face talk to gather information and help; it is useful, but it gives a snapshot, not the year-by-year school record that educational problems need.", "d": "None of the three is wrong because the cumulative record card, option b, is the core tool of educational guidance."}}, {"q": "Reliability is related to:", "concept": "The answer is c: test-retest and odd-even (split-half) are both methods of estimating reliability. Reliability is the consistency of test scores: a reliable test gives about the same result when the same people are tested again, or when equivalent sets of items are compared. It is expressed as a reliability coefficient, a correlation that runs from 0 to 1. In the test-retest method the same test is given twice to the same group after an interval (say two weeks) and the two sets of scores are correlated; this gives the coefficient of stability. In the odd-even method the test is given once, split into odd-numbered and even-numbered items, and the two half-scores are correlated; because each half is only half as long as the full test, the result is stepped up with the Spearman-Brown prophecy formula (Charles Spearman and William Brown, 1910). This is a measure of internal consistency. Other methods are parallel (alternate) forms, the Kuder-Richardson formulas (G. Frederic Kuder and Marion Richardson, 1937), Lee Cronbach's (1951) coefficient alpha and agreement between raters. Example: Ms Lata gives her class of 40 a vocabulary test; correlating each pupil's March and April scores is test-retest, while correlating each pupil's odd-item and even-item totals from one sitting is split-half. Option c is right because each listed method yields a reliability coefficient. Exam trap: validity is a different question (does the test measure the right thing?), and its types are content, criterion-related and construct validity, not retesting or splitting items.", "traps": {"a": "Test-retest is a reliability method (coefficient of stability), but choosing it alone leaves out odd-even, which is also one.", "b": "Odd-even is the split-half method of reliability (internal consistency, corrected with the Spearman-Brown formula), but choosing it alone leaves out test-retest.", "d": "None of the three is wrong because both listed methods estimate reliability, so option c is right."}}, {"q": "Hypochondria is a type of:", "concept": "The answer is b: hypochondria (hypochondriasis) is classed as a psychoneurosis, a neurotic disorder. Hypochondriasis is a persistent preoccupation with having, or getting, a serious illness despite normal medical tests and reassurance; ordinary body sensations (a headache, a fast heartbeat) are read as signs of cancer or heart disease. The person keeps checking the body, searching the internet and visiting doctors, or avoids them out of fear. In classical psychiatry a neurosis (psychoneurosis) is a disorder in which the person suffers distress but keeps insight and contact with reality, with no delusions or hallucinations. The neuroses listed in older texts include anxiety, phobic, obsessive-compulsive, hysterical, neurasthenic, depressive and hypochondriacal neurosis; DSM-II (1968) used the name 'hypochondriacal neurosis'. Psychosis, by contrast, is a loss of contact with reality. Example: Suresh is sure his headaches mean a brain tumour; three normal scans calm him for only a few days, yet he can admit the doctors may be right. Option b is right because the hypochondriac's worry is excessive but not delusional, so it belongs with the neuroses. Today DSM-5 (2013) divides it between somatic symptom disorder and illness anxiety disorder, while ICD-11 keeps the name hypochondriasis under obsessive-compulsive or related disorders. Exam trap: a fixed, unshakeable false belief of disease is a somatic delusion, which is psychotic (as in delusional disorder or psychotic depression); that is not hypochondria.", "traps": {"a": "Psychosis means loss of contact with reality, with delusions or hallucinations; the hypochondriac's fear is excessive, but insight and reality contact are kept, so it is not psychotic.", "c": "Both is wrong because hypochondria is not neurotic and psychotic at once; a fixed false belief of illness would be a somatic delusion, a different, psychotic symptom.", "d": "None of the three is wrong because psychoneurosis, option b, is the classical category for hypochondriasis."}}, {"q": "Tendency to perceive objects as whole entities, despite the fact that some parts may be missing, is a law of:", "concept": "The answer is c: the tendency to see objects as complete wholes even when parts are missing is the Gestalt law of closure. Max Wertheimer, Wolfgang Kohler and Kurt Koffka founded Gestalt psychology in Germany around 1912; they held that the whole is different from the sum of its parts, because the mind organises sensations into meaningful patterns. Wertheimer (1923) set out the laws of perceptual organisation. Closure: we fill in gaps, so a circle drawn with breaks, or the three corners of a triangle, are seen as a full circle or triangle. Gaetano Kanizsa's (1955) illusory triangle, made of three 'Pac-Man' shapes, makes us see a bright triangle that is not drawn at all. Similarity: items alike in colour or shape are grouped together. Proximity: items close together are grouped. Common region: items inside one boundary are grouped (a law added by Stephen Palmer, 1992). All fall under the law of Pragnanz (good form): we see the simplest, most stable organisation. Example: Riya passes a shop sign with one letter burnt out, 'SW_ETS', and still sees 'SWEETS'; her mind closes the gap. Option c is right because filling in missing parts to see a whole is exactly what closure means. Exam trap: continuity (good continuation), seeing smooth lines rather than sudden turns, is often confused with closure; closure completes gaps, continuity follows a path.", "traps": {"a": "Similarity is the Gestalt law that items alike in colour, shape or size are grouped together; it says nothing about filling in missing parts.", "b": "Proximity is the Gestalt law that items close together in space or time are seen as a group, such as dots arranged in pairs.", "d": "Common region, a later law added by Stephen Palmer (1992), says items inside the same boundary are grouped; it does not complete missing parts."}}, {"q": "Cycle driving helps in scooter driving is transfer of learning:", "concept": "The answer is a: when cycling makes it easier to learn to ride a scooter, that is positive transfer of learning. Transfer of learning (transfer of training) is the effect of earlier learning on later learning. It is positive when the earlier learning helps the new task, negative when it interferes, and zero when it has no effect. Cycling and scooter riding share balance, steering, leaning into turns, braking and road sense, so a cyclist learns the scooter faster. Example: Arjun, who has cycled to school for years, rides his father's scooter confidently in two days, while his cousin, who never cycled, takes two weeks. Negative transfer would be a driver used to driving on the left in India struggling at first to drive on the right abroad. Theories of transfer include identical elements (Edward Thorndike and Robert Woodworth, 1901: transfer depends on elements the two tasks share); generalisation (Charles Judd, 1908: boys taught the principle of refraction hit an underwater target better when its depth changed); and transposition (the Gestalt view that relationships, not elements, transfer). Thorndike's work also undermined formal discipline, the belief that Latin or mathematics trains the mind as a whole. Option a is right because the earlier skill helps the new one. Exam trap: positive and negative here mean helping and hindering, not good and bad reinforcement; and bilateral transfer is a different term, meaning transfer from one hand or side of the body to the other.", "traps": {"b": "Negative transfer is when earlier learning interferes with new learning (left-side driving habits hamper right-side driving), the opposite of cycling helping scooter riding.", "c": "Zero transfer means the earlier learning has no effect on the new task, as when learning to swim does nothing for learning Sanskrit grammar.", "d": "None of the three is wrong because the helping effect of cycling on scooter riding is positive transfer, option a."}}, {"q": "Social distance scale is associated with:", "concept": "The answer is a: the social distance scale was devised by Emory S. Bogardus (1925). Social distance is the degree of closeness a person is willing to accept with members of another group (ethnic, national, religious or caste). Bogardus listed seven graded relationships and asked respondents which they would accept for each group: to close kinship by marriage, to my club as personal chums, to my street as neighbours, to employment in my occupation, to citizenship in my country, as visitors only to my country, and would exclude from my country. The closest relationship a person accepts marks his social distance: the farther it is, the stronger the prejudice. It is often called the oldest attitude scale, and it is cumulative: accepting a close level (marriage) usually implies accepting the farther ones (neighbour, citizen). Example: Rahul would accept people of a certain community as co-workers and fellow citizens but not as neighbours or relatives; his answers mark a moderate social distance. Option a is right because the social distance scale is Bogardus's own scale. Exam trap: Louis Thurstone (with Ernest Chave, 1929) built a different attitude scale, the method of equal-appearing intervals, in which judges sort statements into 11 piles; examiners pair the two names because both built classic attitude scales.", "traps": {"b": "Louis Thurstone (1929, with Ernest Chave) devised the method of equal-appearing intervals for measuring attitudes, not the social distance scale.", "c": "Raymond Cattell (1949) built the Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire (16PF), a personality inventory, not an attitude or social distance scale.", "d": "None of the three is wrong because Emory Bogardus, option a, devised the social distance scale."}}, {"q": "Rapport development is necessarily needed for:", "concept": "The answer is d: rapport is necessarily needed for a counsellor, because counselling works through the relationship itself. Rapport is a warm, trusting, comfortable working relationship in which the client feels safe, accepted and understood. In counselling it is the first step and the foundation of everything that follows: without it a client will not reveal fears, failures or family secrets, and no assessment or advice can work. Counsellors build it by greeting warmly, explaining the purpose and the limits of confidentiality, listening closely, starting with neutral topics and never judging. Carl Rogers (1957) set out the conditions he held to be necessary and sufficient for therapeutic change; the three that come from the counsellor, now called the core conditions, are congruence (genuineness), unconditional positive regard and empathic understanding. Example: Pooja, 17, sent to the school counsellor for falling marks, says little at the first meeting; only after the counsellor has listened without blame for two sessions does she reveal that her parents are separating. Option d is right because the counsellor's whole method is the helping relationship, so rapport is essential, not optional. Exam trap: teachers, doctors and managers also work better with rapport, so the other options feel reasonable; but only for the counsellor is it a necessary condition of the work itself. A surgeon can operate well with little rapport; a counsellor cannot counsel without it.", "traps": {"a": "A teacher benefits from good rapport with pupils, but teaching can go on without a deep personal relationship; for the counsellor it is the core of the work.", "b": "A doctor needs patients' trust, but much medical treatment (tests, surgery, medicines) works without close rapport; counselling cannot.", "c": "A manager benefits from good relations with staff, but management rests on authority, planning and organisation, not on a helping relationship."}}, {"q": "TAT and RIB are ______ personality assessment techniques.", "concept": "The answer is c: the Thematic Apperception Test (TAT) and the Rorschach Ink Blot test (RIB) are projective techniques of personality assessment. Projective techniques present ambiguous, unstructured stimuli and ask the person to describe them or tell a story; because the material has no fixed meaning, the person projects his own needs, conflicts, motives and feelings onto it. Lawrence Frank (1939) coined the term 'projective methods'. The TAT, by Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray (1935), has 31 picture cards (one of them blank); Henry Murray's standard procedure gave 20 over two sessions, though many clinicians now use fewer, and the person tells a story for each: what led up to the scene, what is happening, what the characters feel and how it ends. The stories are analysed for needs (such as achievement) and press (forces from the environment). The Rorschach, by Hermann Rorschach (1921, Psychodiagnostik), uses 10 symmetrical inkblots; responses are scored for location, determinants (form, colour, movement, shading) and content, now often with John Exner's (1974) Comprehensive System. Example: shown the TAT card of a boy gazing at a violin, Vikram tells of a boy forced by his father to practise who secretly dreams of running away; the psychologist notes themes of parental pressure and escape. Option c is right because both tests use ambiguous stimuli to draw out hidden aspects of personality. Exam trap: objective tests are structured self-report inventories with fixed answers and objective scoring (MMPI, 16PF, Eysenck Personality Inventory), the opposite of the TAT and Rorschach.", "traps": {"a": "Subjective methods, in Indian textbook classifications, are techniques such as the autobiography, case history and interview, in which the result rests on the person's own account or the assessor's judgement; the TAT and Rorschach do not ask for direct self-description.", "b": "Objective techniques are structured inventories with fixed answer choices and objective scoring, such as the MMPI and the 16PF (some Indian texts also list rating scales and controlled observation here); the TAT and Rorschach use ambiguous material and open responses.", "d": "All the three is wrong because the TAT and Rorschach are neither subjective self-reports nor objective inventories; they are projective."}}, {"q": "In Psychosis a person has ______ contact with reality.", "concept": "The answer is b: in psychosis a person has no contact with reality; loss of reality contact is what separates psychosis from neurosis. Psychosis is a severe mental disorder in which reality testing, the ability to tell inner experience from the outside world, breaks down. Its signs are hallucinations (perceptions without a stimulus), delusions (fixed false beliefs), disorganised thinking and speech, and little or no insight: the person does not see that he is ill. It occurs in schizophrenia, delusional disorder, mania or severe depression with psychotic features, and some organic and drug-induced states. Neurosis is the contrast: the person suffers anxiety, phobias or obsessions but keeps contact with reality and knows the fears are excessive. The standard textbook table reads: neurosis, contact kept and insight present; psychosis, contact lost and insight absent; neurosis is usually treated with counselling or psychotherapy, psychosis usually with medication and sometimes hospital care. Example: Ramesh believes his neighbours have hidden cameras in his ceiling fan and hears them commenting on him; no argument shakes the belief, and he refuses treatment because he says nothing is wrong with him. Option b is right because loss of contact with reality is the defining mark of psychosis. Exam trap: 'full' contact describes the normal person and, broadly, the neurotic. Key note: many people with psychosis keep partial contact (they may know where they are and talk normally while holding a delusion), so 'some' is arguable; but the textbook contrast is that neurosis keeps contact and psychosis loses it, so the answer used here, b, stands.", "traps": {"a": "Some contact fits milder or partial states, but the textbook contrast defines psychosis as a loss of contact with reality, so partial contact is not its defining mark.", "c": "Full contact with reality describes the normal person and the neurotic, who keeps insight; it is the opposite of psychosis.", "d": "None of the three is wrong because the standard answer, no contact with reality, is listed as option b."}}, {"q": "Parental attention to their crying child is:", "concept": "The answer is b: a parent's attention to a crying child is involuntary attention, captured at once without any deliberate effort. Attention is the focusing of consciousness on one object out of many. William James (1890) wrote that attention is either 'passive, reflex, non-voluntary, effortless' or 'active and voluntary', and Indian educational psychology texts keep this two-way split. Voluntary (volitional) attention is deliberate and effortful: we direct it by will toward a goal, as when a student forces himself to concentrate on a dull chapter before an exam; it tires us and needs motivation. Involuntary (non-voluntary) attention is captured without effort or decision, either by the stimulus itself (a loud bang, sudden movement, intense colour, novelty) or by our instincts, emotions and interests. A baby's cry is both: a loud, urgent sound and a signal that stirs the parental instinct, which William McDougall (1908) listed, with its emotion of tenderness, among the human instincts. Example: Sunita is cooking and talking on the phone; the moment her baby cries in the next room, her attention swings to him before she has decided anything. Option b is right because the cry pulls the parent's attention automatically, through stimulus intensity and instinct, not by an act of will. Exam trap: 'voluntary' is tempting because a caring parent wants to attend; but wanting is not effort, and voluntary attention is the effortful kind a student needs for a boring lesson. Key note: a parent can also choose to keep attending, which looks voluntary, and some texts split involuntary (non-volitional) attention into 'enforced' (driven by instinct) and 'spontaneous' (driven by sentiment or interest), so the labels vary; but in the voluntary versus involuntary split the instinct-driven pull of a baby's cry is involuntary, so the answer used here, b, stands.", "traps": {"a": "Voluntary attention is the deliberate, effortful kind, directed by will toward a goal (concentrating on a dull textbook); a crying child captures a parent's attention without such effort.", "c": "Both is wrong because the cry captures attention before any decision is made; a later choice to keep attending does not change how the attention was caught.", "d": "None of the three is wrong because involuntary attention, option b, fits the stem."}}, {"q": "Two-factor theory of Intelligence is associated with:", "concept": "The answer is c: the two-factor theory of intelligence is associated with Charles Spearman. Charles Spearman (1904), a British psychologist, noticed that schoolchildren's marks in very different areas (classics, mathematics, English, even pitch discrimination) all correlated positively: a child good at one tended to be good at the others. He explained this positive manifold with two kinds of factor. The general factor, g, is a common mental energy that enters every intellectual task. The specific factors, s, are narrow abilities, each peculiar to one task. So every test score is made of g plus its own s, and tests correlate with each other because they share g. To show this he developed an early form of factor analysis, and he set out the theory fully in The Abilities of Man (1927). Example: Riya scores high in arithmetic, vocabulary and puzzles; Spearman would credit her strong g for all three, and her own s for the extra edge she shows in arithmetic. Option c is right because 'two-factor' means exactly these two factors, g and s, and the theory carries Spearman's name. Exam trap: the label 'two-factor theory' is reused elsewhere, for Stanley Schachter and Jerome Singer's (1962) theory of emotion, O. Hobart Mowrer's (1947) theory of avoidance learning and Frederick Herzberg's (1959) theory of work motivation; for intelligence it is always Spearman. Examiners also set the multiple-factor theorists beside him: Louis Thurstone (1938) found seven primary abilities, J. P. Guilford (1967) proposed 120, and Edward Thorndike (1920) split intelligence into abstract, mechanical and social.", "traps": {"a": "J. P. Guilford (1967) proposed the Structure of Intellect: 5 operations times 4 contents times 6 products gives 120 separate abilities (later 150 and 180), a many-factor model with no general g.", "b": "Edward Thorndike (1920) held a multifactor view: intelligence is a great many specific connections, grouped as abstract, mechanical and social intelligence, with no single dominant g.", "d": "Louis Thurstone (1938) proposed the multiple-factor (group-factor) theory of seven primary mental abilities, such as verbal comprehension, number, space and reasoning; that is seven group factors, not g plus s."}}, {"q": "A prejudiced person is:", "concept": "The answer is c: a prejudiced person is both biased and blind to reason. Prejudice literally means pre-judgement (Latin prae, before, and judicium, judgement). It is an attitude, usually negative, toward a group and its members, formed before or without adequate evidence and held simply because of group membership. Gordon Allport (1954), in The Nature of Prejudice, called ethnic prejudice 'an antipathy based upon a faulty and inflexible generalization'. Both halves of that phrase match the options. 'Faulty generalization' is bias: the person prejudges every member of a group by a stereotype. 'Inflexible' is blindness to reason: Allport noted that prejudgements become prejudices only when they are not reversible when exposed to new knowledge. Prejudice has three components: cognitive (the stereotype), affective (the dislike) and behavioural (discrimination). Example: Arjun believes all people from a certain region are lazy (bias). He then works beside Kabir from that region, who works harder than anyone in the office, and simply decides Kabir is an exception, keeping his belief intact (blind to reason). Allport called this trick of admitting exceptions while keeping the generalisation 're-fencing'. Option c is right because prejudice needs both the slanted prejudgement and the refusal to revise it in the face of evidence. Exam trap: examiners split the definition into two options so that a student who remembers only one half picks it alone; also keep prejudice (an attitude) apart from discrimination (the action) and a stereotype (the belief).", "traps": {"a": "Biased is half of the definition: the faulty, stereotyped prejudgement of a group, the cognitive side of prejudice. Alone it leaves out the inflexibility, the refusal to change when shown contrary evidence.", "b": "Blind to reason is the other half: the inflexible resistance to new knowledge that, for Gordon Allport, turns a prejudgement into a prejudice. Alone it leaves out the biased, unfavourable judgement of the group itself.", "d": "Impartial means fair and free of bias, the exact opposite of prejudiced; it describes an unprejudiced person."}}, {"q": "Rogers is associated with ______ counselling.", "concept": "The answer is b: Carl Rogers is associated with non-directive counselling. Carl Rogers (1942), in Counseling and Psychotherapy, described a method in which the counsellor does not advise, diagnose, interpret or steer. The client leads, choosing what to talk about, and the counsellor listens, accepts and reflects back the feelings the client expresses, so that the client gains insight and reaches his own decisions. Rogers trusted an inborn actualising tendency: given the right relationship, people grow on their own. He later renamed the approach client-centred (Client-Centered Therapy, 1951) and then person-centred, and in 1957 he named its core conditions: genuineness (congruence), unconditional positive regard and empathic understanding. Example: Meera tells the school counsellor she is confused about leaving the science stream. A non-directive counsellor says, 'You feel torn between what your parents want and what you want,' rather than 'Take commerce.' Option b is right because Rogers' method was named non-directive precisely because the counsellor gives up direction and the responsibility for choices stays with the client. Exam trap: the paired name is Edmund G. Williamson (1939), the Minnesota trait-factor counsellor whose directive, counsellor-centred counselling runs through analysis, synthesis, diagnosis, prognosis, counselling and follow-up. Directive = Williamson; non-directive = Rogers; eclectic = Frederick Thorne (1950), who combined the two as the case required.", "traps": {"a": "Directive counselling is Edmund G. Williamson's (1939) counsellor-centred, trait-factor approach: the counsellor collects data, diagnoses the problem and advises. Rogers built his method as a reaction against it.", "c": "Psychoanalytic counselling comes from Sigmund Freud: the analyst interprets unconscious conflicts through free association, dreams and transference. Rogers rejected this expert-interpreter role.", "d": "All the three cannot be right: directive and non-directive are opposites, and Rogers broke with both directive advice and psychoanalytic interpretation."}}, {"q": "Cattell's Test of Personality has ______ factors.", "concept": "The answer is d: Cattell's personality test, the Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire (16PF), measures 16 factors. Raymond Cattell (1949) built it by factor analysis. He began from the lexical idea that important traits are coded in everyday language, took the list of about 18,000 trait words compiled by Gordon Allport and Henry Odbert (1936), cut it down, and factor-analysed ratings and questionnaire answers. Clusters of traits that simply go together he called surface traits; the deeper factors underneath them he called source traits, and 16 of these form the 16PF. They are labelled by letters: A warmth, B reasoning, C emotional stability, E dominance, F liveliness, G rule-consciousness, H social boldness, I sensitivity, L vigilance, M abstractedness, N privateness, O apprehension, and Q1 to Q4 (openness to change, self-reliance, perfectionism, tension). Scores are reported as stens (1 to 10), and the fifth edition (1993) has 185 items. Example: Kabir's 16PF profile shows high H (socially bold) and high Q2 (self-reliant), which his career counsellor uses when discussing jobs. Option d is right because sixteen source traits are the defining feature of the test, as its very name says. Exam trap: other personality measures carry other numbers, and examiners mix them up: Hans Eysenck's (1975) Eysenck Personality Questionnaire has three superfactors (psychoticism, extraversion, neuroticism), the Big Five has five, and Cattell's own High School Personality Questionnaire for adolescents measures 14 factors.", "traps": {"a": "Ten is not the 16PF's number of factors; the only ten in the 16PF is the sten scale (1 to 10) on which its scores are reported, which may be why it looks familiar.", "b": "Eight is not the 16PF's count; it is only half of the sixteen factors that give Cattell's questionnaire its name.", "c": "Twelve is the number of 16PF factors (lettered A to O) that Cattell found in both life-record (L) data and questionnaire (Q) data; the four Q factors (Q1 to Q4) appeared only in questionnaires, making 16 in all."}}, {"q": "Paranoid personality disorder is characterised by:", "concept": "The answer is c: paranoid personality disorder is marked by both pervasive distrust and suspiciousness of others. DSM-5-TR (2022) defines it as 'a pervasive distrust and suspiciousness of others such that their motives are interpreted as malevolent', beginning by early adulthood and present in many settings. At least four of seven signs are needed: suspecting, without good reason, that others are exploiting, harming or deceiving him; doubts about the loyalty of friends; reluctance to confide for fear the information will be used against him; reading hidden insults or threats into harmless remarks; bearing grudges; seeing attacks on his character that others do not see and counterattacking quickly; and repeated unjustified suspicion that a spouse is unfaithful. It belongs to Cluster A (odd or eccentric), with schizoid and schizotypal personality disorders. Unlike delusional disorder or schizophrenia, the suspicions do not reach the level of fixed delusions and there are no hallucinations. Example: Vikram is sure his colleagues are plotting to get him sacked, will not tell a close friend his salary in case it is used against him, and still holds a grudge over a joke made years ago. Option c is right because the core criterion joins the two words: distrust and suspiciousness are both named in the same defining sentence. Exam trap: examiners offer one word of the pair as a separate option; also do not confuse paranoid personality (a lasting trait pattern without delusions) with delusional disorder, persecutory type, or with the old paranoid subtype of schizophrenia (dropped in DSM-5).", "traps": {"a": "Pervasive distrust is the first half of the DSM-5-TR core phrase; on its own it leaves out the suspiciousness of others' motives that the same sentence names.", "b": "Suspiciousness of others is the second half of the same phrase; alone it leaves out the pervasive distrust that colours all the person's relationships.", "d": "None of the three is wrong because both a and b come straight from the DSM-5-TR definition, so c is right."}}, {"q": "Median is a measure of:", "concept": "The answer is a: the median is a measure of central tendency. A measure of central tendency is a single score that represents the centre, the typical value, of a set of scores. There are three: the mean (the arithmetic average), the median and the mode (the most frequent score). The median is the middle score when all the scores are arranged in order: half the group lies above it and half below, so it is also the 50th percentile and the second quartile (Q2). With an odd number of scores it is the middle one; with an even number it is the average of the two middle ones. Its great strength is that extreme scores do not pull it, so it is preferred for skewed data such as income, and for open-ended classes. Example: Riya's group of five scores 3, 5, 7, 9 and 40 on a quiz; the median is 7, but the mean is 12.8, dragged up by the one very high score, so the median describes the typical student better. Option a is right because the median locates the centre of a distribution, not its spread and not a forecast. Exam trap: the median is often confused with the quartile deviation, which uses Q1 and Q3 to measure spread; both come from the same ordered list of scores, but only the median (Q2) is a measure of central tendency.", "traps": {"b": "Variability (dispersion) is measured by the range, quartile deviation, mean deviation, standard deviation and variance; these show how spread out the scores are, not where their centre lies.", "c": "Prediction of one variable from another is the job of regression (built on correlation), not of a single summary score such as the median.", "d": "All the three fails because the median answers only one question, where the middle of the scores lies; spread and prediction need other statistics."}}, {"q": "Convergent thinking is same as:", "concept": "The answer is b: convergent thinking is the kind of thinking intelligence tests measure, so of these options it matches intelligence. J. P. Guilford (1950), in his presidential address to the American Psychological Association, and later in his Structure of Intellect model (1967), separated two mental operations. Convergent production (convergent thinking) narrows information down to the single correct answer, as in an IQ test item, a maths sum or a multiple-choice question. Divergent production (divergent thinking) spreads out to many varied, original answers to an open problem; it is the core of creativity and is scored for fluency (how many ideas), flexibility (how many kinds), originality (how rare) and elaboration (how detailed). It is tested by tasks such as listing all the uses of a brick and by the Tests of Creative Thinking of E. Paul Torrance (1966). Example: asked 'What is 7 times 8?', Arjun converges on 56; asked 'How many uses can you think of for a newspaper?', he diverges: wrapping, a fan, a paper boat, a hat. Option b is right because standard intelligence tests reward convergent thinking, while creativity rests on divergent thinking. Exam trap: students swap the pair; remember that convergent means coming together on one answer (intelligence) and divergent means spreading out to many answers (creativity). Key note: convergent thinking is only one part of intelligence, not literally the same thing, but of the options intelligence is its match while creativity is divergent thinking, so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Creativity rests on divergent thinking, J. P. Guilford's divergent production of many novel answers, which is the opposite of converging on one correct answer.", "c": "Both cannot be right because creativity is divergent, not convergent; only intelligence matches convergent thinking.", "d": "None of the three is wrong because intelligence, option b, is the standard match for convergent thinking."}}, {"q": "The most useful communication for Blind Children is:", "concept": "The answer is b: verbal communication is the most useful channel for blind children. Communication is either verbal (words, spoken or written) or non-verbal (everything else: facial expression, gesture, eye contact, posture, distance, touch and tone of voice). Most non-verbal cues are visual, so a child who cannot see misses a smile, a nod, a frown or a pointing finger. Spoken language and careful listening therefore carry most of a blind child's learning and social contact, and Braille, the raised-dot script devised by Louis Braille (1824), gives a verbal code for reading and writing through the fingertips. Teachers of visually impaired children are trained to describe actions aloud, call the child by name before speaking, say who has entered the room, and give spoken feedback instead of a nod. Example: in Meera's class the teacher says, 'Meera, the map is on your desk; India is under your right hand,' rather than pointing at it. Option b is right because words reach a blind child fully, while most non-verbal signals do not reach him at all. Exam trap: 'non-verbal' sounds gentler and more natural, so it tempts; for deaf children the stress does shift to visual channels such as sign and lip-reading, but for blind children it is verbal. Key note: touch is non-verbal and does matter for blind children, so a or c has a case; but most non-verbal channels are visual and lost to them, and language is their main channel, so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Non-verbal communication (facial expression, gesture, eye contact, posture) is mostly visual, so a blind child cannot receive most of it; only touch and tone of voice remain.", "c": "Both overstates the non-verbal side: touch and tone help, but most non-verbal channels are visual and closed to a blind child, so they are not equally useful.", "d": "None of the three is wrong because verbal communication, option b, is the main channel for blind children."}}, {"q": "Teacher is to student as Counsellor is to:", "concept": "The answer is c: the person a counsellor helps is called both a counselee and a client. The analogy works like this: a teacher teaches a student, and a counsellor counsels a counselee, also called a client. Both terms are standard. 'Counselee' (the one who is counselled) is the usual word in school guidance texts, especially in accounts of directive counselling, the approach of Edmund G. Williamson (1939) in which the counsellor, rather like a teacher, collects data, diagnoses the problem and advises. 'Client' was made standard by Carl Rogers (1951), whose approach was named client-centred therapy: he preferred it to 'patient' to stress that the person is an active, responsible adult who seeks help by choice, not a sick person waiting to be treated. Indian guidance and counselling texts use the two words interchangeably for the same person. Example: when Kabir, a Class 12 pupil, meets the school counsellor about choosing a career, the books may call him the counselee or the client; the meaning is the same. Option c is right because both words name the person on the receiving end of counselling, just as 'student' names the person on the receiving end of teaching. Exam trap: examiners split two synonyms into separate options so that a student who knows only one of them picks it alone; a third word, 'patient', belongs to medicine and psychiatry and is avoided in counselling.", "traps": {"a": "Counselee is correct but only half the answer: it is the usual guidance-text term, and choosing it alone leaves out the equally standard 'client'.", "b": "Client is correct but only half the answer: it is Carl Rogers' preferred term, and choosing it alone leaves out 'counselee', which guidance texts use for the same person.", "d": "None of the three is wrong because both counselee and client name the person a counsellor helps, so c is right."}}, {"q": "Perfect correlation is:", "concept": "The answer is c: perfect correlation is 1.0 (that is, 1.00). The correlation coefficient tells how closely two variables move together. Karl Pearson's (1896) product-moment coefficient, r, runs from minus 1.00 through 0 to plus 1.00. The sign shows direction: plus means both rise together (height and weight), minus means one rises as the other falls (days absent and marks). The size, ignoring the sign, shows strength. A perfect correlation is r = plus 1.00 (perfect positive) or minus 1.00 (perfect negative): every point on the scatterplot lies exactly on a straight line, so one variable predicts the other without error. Zero means no straight-line relationship. As a rough guide, 0.9 is very high, 0.8 high and 0.5 moderate; real psychological data are almost never perfect. Example: if Riya is paid exactly 100 rupees for every hour she works, her hours and her pay correlate plus 1.00; her hours of study and her exam marks might correlate about 0.5, because many other things affect marks. Option c is right because only 1.00 means the relationship holds without a single exception. Exam trap: a very high r such as 0.9 tempts students, but high is not perfect; and minus 1.00 is just as perfect as plus 1.00, because the sign shows only direction, not strength. Key note: the paper prints '01', a slip for 1 (that is, 1.00); read that way c stands, and perfect negative correlation is minus 1.00.", "traps": {"a": "0.9 is a very high positive correlation, but some points still fall off the straight line, so it is not perfect.", "b": "0.8 is a high positive correlation, strong but well short of the perfect plus 1.00.", "d": "0.5 is a moderate correlation: the two variables are related, but with many exceptions."}}, {"q": "Mentally retarded is/are:", "concept": "The answer is d: morons, imbeciles and idiots were all old labels for levels of mental retardation. Early in the twentieth century, mental deficiency was graded by severity, roughly by IQ: idiot (IQ below about 25, needing total lifelong care), imbecile (about 25 to 50, able to learn self-care and simple tasks under supervision) and moron (about 50 to 70, able to learn basic school skills and simple work). Henry Goddard (1910) coined 'moron' from a Greek word meaning foolish. Over time all three words turned into insults and were dropped. They were replaced first by mild, moderate, severe and profound mental retardation (in DSM-IV, mild ran from an IQ of 50 to 55 up to about 70), and then by intellectual disability (intellectual developmental disorder) in DSM-5 (2013), where severity is judged by adaptive functioning, not by IQ alone; ICD-11 uses disorders of intellectual development. Schools also used the labels educable, trainable and custodial. Example: an old hospital record describing Kabir's great-uncle as an 'imbecile' would today read moderate intellectual disability. Option d is right because each word named one band of the same condition, from mildest (moron) to most severe (idiot). Exam trap: students pick moron alone because it is the best-known word, or think idiot and imbecile are everyday insults rather than technical grades; all three were levels on one scale. Learn the old terms only to recognise them in exams, and never use them with clients or families.", "traps": {"a": "Moron was the mildest band (IQ about 50 to 70, Henry Goddard's 1910 term); correct, but alone it leaves out the moderate and severe bands.", "b": "Imbecile was the middle band (IQ about 25 to 50); correct, but alone it leaves out morons and idiots.", "c": "Idiot was the most severe band (IQ below about 25, needing total care); correct, but alone it leaves out the milder bands."}}, {"q": "SD in ungrouped data is computed by:", "concept": "The answer is b: for ungrouped data the standard deviation is SD = √(Σx² / N), where x = X minus M, the deviation of each score from the mean. The SD tells how far scores typically lie from the mean; it is the most widely used measure of variability, and Karl Pearson (1894) gave it its name. The steps follow the formula from the inside out. Find the mean (M). Subtract it from each score to get x. Square each x, which removes the minus signs and gives extra weight to large deviations. Add the squares to get Σx². Divide by N, which gives the variance, the mean of the squared deviations. Finally take the square root, which brings the answer back to the original units of measurement. Example: Meera's three test scores are 2, 4 and 6. The mean is 4; the deviations are minus 2, 0 and plus 2; the squares are 4, 0 and 4; Σx² = 8; 8 divided by 3 = 2.67, the variance; the square root of 2.67 = 1.63, the SD. When a sample is used to estimate the population SD, many books divide by N minus 1 instead of N. Option b is right because the SD is the square root of the variance, and only option b takes that root. Exam trap: option a, Σx² / N, is a real formula, but it is the variance (the SD squared), so students who forget the final square root pick it.", "traps": {"a": "Σx² / N is the variance, the mean of the squared deviations; it is in squared units and must be square-rooted to give the SD.", "c": "√Σx / N is meaningless as a measure of spread: deviations from the mean always add up to zero (Σx = 0), so it would always give zero.", "d": "None of the three is wrong because option b is the standard SD formula for ungrouped data."}}, {"q": "Psychoanalysis is related to:", "concept": "The answer is d: the id, the ego and the superego are all concepts of psychoanalysis. Psychoanalysis is both the theory of personality and the method of therapy founded by Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939). In The Ego and the Id (1923) he proposed his structural model of the mind, with three systems. The id is present at birth and wholly unconscious, the store of instinctual drives (the life instincts, including sex, and the death or aggressive instincts); it works on the pleasure principle, seeking instant gratification through primary process thinking, the wish-fulfilling imagery seen in dreams. The ego grows out of the id to deal with the real world; it works on the reality principle and secondary process (realistic, logical) thinking, mediates between id, superego and reality, and uses defence mechanisms when anxiety rises. The superego is the internalised voice of parents and society, formed around age 5 as the Oedipus complex resolves; its two parts are the conscience, which punishes with guilt, and the ego ideal, which rewards with pride. Example: hungry Arjun sees a classmate's tiffin; his id says 'grab it', his superego says 'stealing is wrong', and his ego decides to ask politely for a share. Option d is right because all three structures belong to the same psychoanalytic model. Exam trap: the earlier topographic model (1900: conscious, preconscious, unconscious) is a different Freudian model, and examiners also swap the principles: pleasure = id, reality = ego, morality = superego.", "traps": {"a": "The id is the inborn, unconscious seat of the drives, working on the pleasure principle; it is a psychoanalytic concept, but alone it leaves out the ego and superego.", "b": "The ego is the reality-based mediator that uses defence mechanisms; it is psychoanalytic, but alone it leaves out the id and superego.", "c": "The superego is the internalised conscience and ego ideal; it is psychoanalytic, but alone it leaves out the id and ego."}}, {"q": "Air pollution is highest in:", "concept": "The answer is d: outdoor air pollution is highest in cities. Air pollution is the presence in the air of harmful gases and particles: fine particulate matter (PM2.5 and PM10), nitrogen dioxide, sulphur dioxide, carbon monoxide and ground-level ozone. Cities concentrate every major source in a small area: heavy vehicle traffic, factories and power plants, construction and road dust, burning of waste, and millions of people living close together, often among tall buildings that trap still air. So pollution rises along the settlement ladder: small habitations and villages have few sources and plenty of open space, towns have more, and cities the most. Data from the World Health Organization and the IQAir rankings regularly place Indian cities such as Delhi among the most polluted in the world, especially in winter, when smoke from crop-stubble burning in neighbouring states adds to the city's own load. Environmental psychology links poor air not only to lung and heart disease but also to lower mood, poorer attention and thinking, and more irritability and aggression. Example: Kabir moves from his village in Punjab to Delhi for college and notices burning eyes and a grey haze every November. Option d is right because cities have the densest concentration of pollution sources. Exam trap: the options run from the smallest settlement to the largest, and examiners hope students will think of rural smoke; outdoor pollution grows with size, so the largest, the city, is the answer. Key note: the paper prints 'Air population', a slip for air pollution. Indoor smoke from cooking fires is a serious rural problem, but outdoor air pollution is highest in cities, and if the word meant population, cities are highest too, so d stands either way.", "traps": {"a": "Habitations are small clusters of homes, often hamlets; they have few vehicles or factories, so their outdoor air is among the cleanest.", "b": "Villages have open space and few industrial sources; indoor smoke from cooking fires is a real rural problem, but overall outdoor pollution is far lower than in cities.", "c": "Towns have more traffic and industry than villages but far less than cities, so they sit in between, not at the top."}}, {"q": "Remedial classes are needed for:", "concept": "The answer is b: remedial classes are needed for underachievers. Remedial teaching is special instruction that finds and corrects specific weaknesses in pupils whose achievement falls below their ability or below the level of their class. An underachiever is a pupil whose actual achievement is clearly lower than his measured ability (IQ or aptitude) predicts: the potential is there, but something blocks it, such as gaps in basic skills, poor study habits, a specific learning disability, low motivation, anxiety or problems at home. Remedial work follows a sequence: a diagnostic test pinpoints exactly what the child gets wrong (which kinds of subtraction, which letter sounds), targeted teaching in small groups then fixes those gaps, and a re-test checks progress. Slow learners and children with learning disabilities also receive remedial help. Gifted and highly intelligent pupils need the opposite kind of provision: enrichment (deeper, broader work), acceleration (moving ahead faster) or special programmes, because ordinary lessons are too easy for them. Example: Riya has an IQ of 115 but keeps failing maths because she never understood place value; after a diagnostic test, remedial lessons on place value lift her marks to match her ability. Option b is right because remedial classes exist to close the gap between ability and achievement, and that gap is the definition of underachievement. Exam trap: a gifted pupil can also underachieve (a gifted underachiever), and then remedial help is needed for the underachievement; the label 'gifted' on its own calls for enrichment, not remediation.", "traps": {"a": "Gifted pupils have exceptional ability and need enrichment or acceleration, not remedial teaching; remediation fills gaps, and giftedness is not a gap.", "c": "Intelligent pupils who achieve at their level are already learning well; they too need enrichment, not remedial classes.", "d": "Both (A) and (C) names two groups who need enrichment rather than remediation, so neither part fits; underachievers alone need remedial classes."}}, {"q": "Creativity and Intelligence have a ______ relationship.", "concept": "The answer is a: creativity and intelligence have a positive relationship, though a modest one. Intelligence tests reward convergent thinking (one right answer), while creativity tests reward divergent thinking (many original answers), a split drawn by J. P. Guilford (1950). If the two were the same ability they would correlate almost perfectly; if they were opposed, the correlation would be negative. Research finds neither. Kyung Hee Kim (2005), pooling many studies of creativity test scores and IQ in a meta-analysis, found an average correlation of about 0.17: positive, but small, so IQ accounts for only about 3% of the differences in creativity scores. The threshold hypothesis adds that the two rise together up to an IQ of about 120, and that above that level extra intelligence adds little to creativity. Jacob Getzels and Philip Jackson (1962) found that highly creative and highly intelligent adolescents were largely different groups, and Michael Wallach and Nathan Kogan (1965) found the two nearly independent when creativity tests were given in a relaxed, game-like way. So some intelligence helps creativity, but a high IQ does not guarantee it. Example: Arjun (IQ 135) solves puzzles quickly but writes ordinary stories, while Meera (IQ 112) produces the most original ideas in her class. Option a is right because the overall correlation is above zero. Exam trap: 'positive' does not mean 'strong'; a small positive correlation is still positive, and 'perfect' would mean the two abilities are identical. Key note: the link is weak, and close to zero above about IQ 120, so some answer d; but the overall correlation is positive (it is neither perfect nor negative), so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Perfect (r = 1.00) would mean creativity and intelligence are one and the same ability; they are clearly distinct, since many high-IQ people are not especially creative.", "c": "Negative would mean brighter people are less creative; studies find the opposite, a small positive link.", "d": "None of the three is wrong because the observed correlation, though weak, is positive, so a is right."}}, {"q": "Delinquents:", "concept": "The answer is b: delinquents violate social norms. Juvenile delinquency is behaviour by a minor that breaks the law or goes against the accepted norms of society: theft, vandalism, fighting, drug use and, in some legal systems, status offences such as truancy or running away from home, which are offences only because of the child's age. In India the Juvenile Justice (Care and Protection of Children) Act, 2015 calls a person under 18 who is alleged to have committed an offence a 'child in conflict with law' and his case goes first to a Juvenile Justice Board, not an ordinary criminal court. Sociologists explain delinquency in several ways: the strain (anomie) theory of Robert Merton (1938), in which blocked legitimate goals push youth toward illegitimate means; the differential association theory of Edwin Sutherland (1939), in which delinquency is learned from close associates; and the delinquent subculture of Albert Cohen (1955). The clinical counterpart is conduct disorder, a repetitive pattern of violating the rights of others or major age-appropriate norms. Example: Rahul, 15, steals mobile phones from a market with older friends; his acts break both the law and the norms of his community. Option b is right because norm violation is the defining feature shared by every delinquent act. Exam trap: maintaining norms is conformity, the opposite; and wandering is only one behaviour some delinquents show, not the definition. Key note: some texts list vagrancy and running away from home as delinquent acts, so d is tempting; but the defining feature, true of every delinquent, is violating social norms, so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Maintaining social norms is conformity, the exact opposite of delinquency.", "c": "Wandering (vagrancy, running away, truancy) is one behaviour some delinquents show, and some texts list it, but many delinquents never wander, so it does not define them.", "d": "Both (B) and (C) treats wandering as defining; norm violation is true of all delinquents, wandering only of some."}}, {"q": "Sensation is related to:", "concept": "The answer is d: sensation is related to the skin, the eyes and ears, and the nose and tongue, all three. Sensation is the process by which sense organs detect physical energy from the world or from the body and convert it into nerve impulses (transduction) that travel to the brain; perception is the next step, in which the brain organises and interprets those signals. Each sense has its own receptors: the eyes (rods and cones in the retina) respond to light, giving vision; the ears (hair cells in the cochlea) respond to sound waves, giving hearing; the nose (olfactory receptors) responds to airborne chemicals, giving smell; the tongue (taste buds) responds to dissolved chemicals, giving taste; and the skin holds receptors for touch, pressure, warmth, cold and pain. These are the five classic senses, and psychology adds the kinaesthetic sense (body position and movement, from receptors in muscles and joints) and the vestibular sense (balance, from the inner ear). Example: at a wedding, Meera sees the lights (eyes), hears the band (ears), smells the jasmine (nose), tastes the sweets (tongue) and feels the warmth of a diya near her hand (skin): five sensations at once. Option d is right because every listed organ houses sensory receptors. Exam trap: students sometimes count only the 'higher' senses of sight and hearing, or think of the skin as a mere covering; the skin is the largest sense organ and carries several separate senses.", "traps": {"a": "The skin is a sense organ (touch, pressure, temperature, pain), but alone it leaves out vision, hearing, smell and taste.", "b": "The eyes and ears give vision and hearing, but alone they leave out the skin senses, smell and taste.", "c": "The nose and tongue give smell and taste (the chemical senses), but alone they leave out the skin, eyes and ears."}}, {"q": "Self-actualisation is associated with:", "concept": "The answer is b: self-actualisation is associated with Abraham Maslow. Abraham Maslow (1943), in 'A Theory of Human Motivation', arranged human needs in a hierarchy: physiological, safety, love and belongingness, esteem, and at the top self-actualisation, the need to become everything one is capable of becoming. In his words, 'What a man can be, he must be.' The lower (deficiency) needs must be fairly well met before the growth need of self-actualisation drives behaviour. In Motivation and Personality (1954) he described self-actualisers, a small, hand-picked group including Abraham Lincoln, Albert Einstein and Eleanor Roosevelt: realistic, accepting, spontaneous, problem-centred and independent, and prone to peak experiences, moments of intense joy and awe. He called humanistic psychology the 'third force', after psychoanalysis and behaviourism. Example: Kabir has a secure job and a loving family, yet feels restless until he starts teaching music to village children, the work he feels he was born to do; that is self-actualisation. Option b is right because self-actualisation is the peak of Maslow's hierarchy and the concept most tied to his name. Exam trap: Kurt Goldstein (1939) coined the term, and Carl Rogers (1951) built his theory on an actualising tendency; examiners use both to pull students away from Maslow. Key note: Kurt Goldstein coined the term and Carl Rogers built his theory on the actualising tendency, so a has a case; but the name tied to self-actualisation in textbooks is Maslow's, at the top of his hierarchy, so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Carl Rogers (1951) described the actualising tendency, an inborn drive toward growth, and the fully functioning person; related, but the term self-actualisation and the hierarchy of needs belong to Maslow.", "c": "Sigmund Freud gave the pleasure principle and the id, ego and superego; his model is driven by instincts and conflict, not by growth toward self-actualisation.", "d": "None of the three is wrong because Abraham Maslow, option b, is the theorist of self-actualisation."}}, {"q": "Social Loafing is associated with:", "concept": "The answer is b: social loafing is associated with group projects. Social loafing is the tendency of people to put in less effort when they work together on a group task, with their contributions pooled so that no one's share can be seen, than when they work alone. Max Ringelmann (1913), a French agricultural engineer, first noticed it: when men pulled on a rope, the force each one exerted fell as the team grew. Part of the loss was poor coordination, but Bibb Latané, Kipling Williams and Stephen Harkins (1979) showed that motivation falls too: students asked to shout and clap as loudly as they could made less noise each as the group grew, even when they only believed others were shouting with them. Steven Karau and Kipling Williams (1993) reviewed the studies in a meta-analysis: loafing shrinks when each person's work can be identified and evaluated, when the task matters, and when the group is cohesive, and it is weaker in collectivist cultures and among women. Example: in a four-member college project, Arjun does almost nothing because only one group mark will be given; if each member's section were graded separately, he would work harder. Option b is right because loafing needs a group task with pooled, unidentifiable effort. Exam trap: do not confuse social loafing with social facilitation (Norman Triplett, 1898; Robert Zajonc, 1965), in which the mere presence of others, with each person's performance visible, speeds up well-learned tasks.", "traps": {"a": "Individual projects make each person's effort visible and evaluated, the very condition that removes social loafing.", "c": "Both is wrong because loafing cannot occur when a person works alone: there is no group in which to hide one's effort.", "d": "None of the three is wrong because group projects, option b, are the classic setting for social loafing."}}]});
